WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:14.030
Hi everyone, thank you so much for being here.

00:00:14.030 --> 00:00:16.100
I am really excited to be here.

00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:18.300
You have a great panel up ahead.

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:25.340
And as I was preparing for it you know it's
called My Society, My Self and I thought well

00:00:25.340 --> 00:00:30.500
how did-how did I define myself and I started
thinking you know what is what's the first

00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:34.410
question I remember asking myself when I was
young?

00:00:34.410 --> 00:00:40.160
And I couldn't remember but I could remember
the earliest questions that others asked me

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:43.400
in the earliest probably, is what's your name?

00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:44.400
Right.

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:46.250
How do you define yourself that way.

00:00:46.250 --> 00:00:47.340
How old are you?

00:00:47.340 --> 00:00:48.870
How old are you little girl?

00:00:48.870 --> 00:00:50.770
I remember those as well.

00:00:50.770 --> 00:00:55.910
And so it's very interesting that my first
kind of perceptions of self came from questions

00:00:55.910 --> 00:00:57.380
that others posed to me.

00:00:57.380 --> 00:00:58.840
What do you want to be when you grow up?

00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:03.320
That's one that I definitely remember and
I'm still asking myself that.

00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:09.170
And these sorts of questions are part of identity,
part of our self.

00:01:09.170 --> 00:01:15.729
Part of the really fascinating things that
this panel is going to be talking about and

00:01:15.729 --> 00:01:17.729
we have such a great group.

00:01:17.729 --> 00:01:23.979
We have perspectives from neuroscience, from
philosophy, from psychology.

00:01:23.979 --> 00:01:31.049
So it's going to be a really fascinating way
to get a lot of different insight into what

00:01:31.049 --> 00:01:32.310
makes us who we are.

00:01:32.310 --> 00:01:34.840
You know what is it that defines human beings?

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:39.389
And what is it that defines how you think
of yourself in your own mind?

00:01:39.389 --> 00:01:42.529
So without further ado let's introduce our
panelists.

00:01:42.529 --> 00:01:48.299
Our first participant is a wonderful neuroscientist
and she's a professor in the Department of

00:01:48.299 --> 00:01:52.840
Psychology and the Zuckerman Mind Brain Behavior
Institute at Columbia.

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:59.359
She researches memory and she studies patients
with brain disorders, Parkinson's, schizophrenia,

00:01:59.359 --> 00:02:05.429
eating disorders and her work has yielded
insights into how the brain changes learning

00:02:05.429 --> 00:02:11.039
how memories are created and retrieved and
how memory shapes our decisions, our actions,

00:02:11.039 --> 00:02:12.039
and ourselves.

00:02:12.039 --> 00:02:13.840
So please give a big welcome to the show.

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:23.980
Come our next participant is a philosopher
and he's from the City University of New York.

00:02:23.980 --> 00:02:29.739
He's a leader in the field of experimental
philosophy and experimental philosophy, will

00:02:29.739 --> 00:02:35.060
talk a little bit about what that is, but
it brings empirical methods to mind on philosophical

00:02:35.060 --> 00:02:36.060
questions.

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:40.540
He is the author of several books including
Furnishing the Mind, Gut Reactions and The

00:02:40.540 --> 00:02:43.920
Emotional Construction of Morals.

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:49.950
So let's please give a round of applause to
Jesse Prinz.

00:02:49.950 --> 00:02:53.590
Also joining us is a post-doctoral fellow
at Yale University.

00:02:53.590 --> 00:02:57.230
She has appointments at the School of Management
and the cognitive science program.

00:02:57.230 --> 00:03:02.459
She conducts research on moral psychology,
personal identity, and emotion and her recent

00:03:02.459 --> 00:03:07.930
work examines how humans create a sense of
continuous identity for themselves and others.

00:03:07.930 --> 00:03:11.030
Please welcome Nina Strohminger.

00:03:11.030 --> 00:03:19.569
And our final participant is a professor of
psychology philosophy and Linguistics at Yale

00:03:19.569 --> 00:03:24.500
University and his work involves using the
kinds of experimental methods associated with

00:03:24.500 --> 00:03:28.969
cognitive science to address the kinds of
questions associated with philosophy.

00:03:28.969 --> 00:03:32.849
He is the coeditor of the book Experimental
Philosophy.

00:03:32.849 --> 00:03:38.439
So please welcome our final participant Joshua
Knobe.

00:03:38.439 --> 00:03:41.579
Thank you guys so much for being here today.

00:03:41.579 --> 00:03:45.959
And I wanted to start since we're going to
be talking a lot about the self with kind

00:03:45.959 --> 00:03:48.680
of the fundamental question, What is the self?

00:03:48.680 --> 00:03:49.749
How do you guys think about it?

00:03:49.749 --> 00:03:50.859
How do you define it?

00:03:50.859 --> 00:03:58.519
So maybe we can start with Josh and work our
way backwards to get to get an insight into

00:03:58.519 --> 00:03:59.870
what the self is.

00:03:59.870 --> 00:04:04.099
Your question is a really good one and I think
one of the things that we've learned over

00:04:04.099 --> 00:04:08.040
these past few years of really trying to study
how people understand the self is that there

00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:09.870
isn't a single way that they understand the
self.

00:04:09.870 --> 00:04:13.489
It's not as though there's some one the thing
that we could study and way well we're studying

00:04:13.489 --> 00:04:16.400
this we're studying how people ordinarily
think of themselves.

00:04:16.400 --> 00:04:19.329
Rather people seem to think about the stuff
in a whole variety of different ways, often

00:04:19.329 --> 00:04:22.800
varying the way they think about the self
depending on the context.

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:25.900
So for example, something that we've been
interested in our own work is just the idea

00:04:25.900 --> 00:04:28.780
that in some cases people think well there
are all sorts of different things that make

00:04:28.780 --> 00:04:29.780
up yourself.

00:04:29.780 --> 00:04:32.600
All sorts of different emotions, beliefs,
desires, and intentions.

00:04:32.600 --> 00:04:35.850
But at other times people end up thinking
about it in a very different way.

00:04:35.850 --> 00:04:39.800
They think, no there's something that's the
core of who you really are and your true self.

00:04:39.800 --> 00:04:43.960
The thing that you really are deep down inside
and then of course there may be these various

00:04:43.960 --> 00:04:50.130
emotions and beliefs that in some sense you
have but those things aren't the real you.

00:04:50.130 --> 00:04:56.140
Well I, Josh and I work together so naturally
we agree on everything.

00:04:56.140 --> 00:05:04.320
He sort of stole my thunder but I guess I'll
just add to that that the work that actually

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:09.810
most people on this panel have been doing
integrates this view that to philosophers

00:05:09.810 --> 00:05:14.290
or people a priori before they've collect
collected any data at all they might say,

00:05:14.290 --> 00:05:17.150
well the self is this or that or this other
thing.

00:05:17.150 --> 00:05:23.790
But the sort of work that we've been engaged
in actually takes work like uses as its definition

00:05:23.790 --> 00:05:27.000
for the self what other people think of the
self as.

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:31.070
So we're not sort of coming down from above
and just giving a definition to it, but rather

00:05:31.070 --> 00:05:35.590
saying like well how do ordinary folk and
ordinary folk think about the self and using

00:05:35.590 --> 00:05:38.600
that to fuel our theories about it.

00:05:38.600 --> 00:05:44.630
Also-let me just add, I mean why are we here
today?

00:05:44.630 --> 00:05:50.110
And I think the interest in this show is symptomatic
of a kind of Western individualism.

00:05:50.110 --> 00:05:55.020
The very word "self" makes us think about
our own individual strivings and goals.

00:05:55.020 --> 00:05:59.800
How do we organize as unique persons isolated
from the rest.

00:05:59.800 --> 00:06:01.080
I think that's fundamentally wrong.

00:06:01.080 --> 00:06:05.780
I think one thing we're learning about the
self is that the self is really at its core

00:06:05.780 --> 00:06:06.990
a social phenomenon.

00:06:06.990 --> 00:06:12.110
The reason why we have self, the reason why
we keep track of identity over time is that

00:06:12.110 --> 00:06:17.380
we need to relate to others in forming those
bonds, those relationships, those social groups

00:06:17.380 --> 00:06:21.420
depends on a certain kind of taxonomy of persons.

00:06:21.420 --> 00:06:25.290
So I don't work with any of these folks and
I totally disagree.

00:06:25.290 --> 00:06:26.290
No.

00:06:26.290 --> 00:06:32.190
But I do have what I think in many ways a
very different perspective as a neuroscientist

00:06:32.190 --> 00:06:38.350
and it is a challenging one in the sense that
I think we'd all probably agree that to the

00:06:38.350 --> 00:06:40.660
extent that we have a sense of self or there
is a self.

00:06:40.660 --> 00:06:43.140
It's not you know there in our knee or our
toes.

00:06:43.140 --> 00:06:49.770
It's got to be in our brain and emerging and
related to biological and neural processes.

00:06:49.770 --> 00:06:54.890
I think that raises a whole set of questions
and answers that are somewhat parallel or

00:06:54.890 --> 00:07:00.910
orthogonal to the perspectives that you guys
mentioned in terms of what does it mean to

00:07:00.910 --> 00:07:01.910
have a self?

00:07:01.910 --> 00:07:04.400
Is a self a changing entity or not?

00:07:04.400 --> 00:07:08.710
And how does it relate to kind of social construction?

00:07:08.710 --> 00:07:12.840
But I think you know it helps I think situate
the question of self, thinking about it as

00:07:12.840 --> 00:07:17.880
it relates to the brain in the context of
similar questions like, What does it mean

00:07:17.880 --> 00:07:20.440
to produce an action in a given moment?

00:07:20.440 --> 00:07:25.560
To what extent does each action we produce
represent our self and inform our self?

00:07:25.560 --> 00:07:28.410
What does it mean to retrieve a memory of
ourselves?

00:07:28.410 --> 00:07:33.490
And so these are the kinds of questions that
I think neuroscience has very specific answers

00:07:33.490 --> 00:07:34.900
to.

00:07:34.900 --> 00:07:43.210
And I think a big challenge is to is to figure
out how those different slices of behavior

00:07:43.210 --> 00:07:47.650
tell us about the self and inform ourselves,
our sense of how the self is constructed.

00:07:47.650 --> 00:07:56.090
So let me follow up on that and ask so what-when
you're when you're talking about the self

00:07:56.090 --> 00:08:00.850
and neuroscience, what are the sorts of questions
that you think that neuroscience can actually

00:08:00.850 --> 00:08:01.850
help us answer?

00:08:01.850 --> 00:08:02.850
And then what.

00:08:02.850 --> 00:08:08.260
You know I love to hear the reaction of the
rest of the rest of you non-neuroscientist

00:08:08.260 --> 00:08:13.010
who all worked together and we'll be speaking
with one voice.

00:08:13.010 --> 00:08:16.130
We will get you to disagree.

00:08:16.130 --> 00:08:21.270
So I'd love to kind of hear you guys discuss
a little bit about what methods might be appropriate

00:08:21.270 --> 00:08:26.340
to what sorts of questions and do we need
all of them to actually figure out what the

00:08:26.340 --> 00:08:27.340
self is?

00:08:27.340 --> 00:08:31.030
Right maybe you know just start by making
a statement that might sound a little blasphemous

00:08:31.030 --> 00:08:34.070
coming from the neuroscientists which is I
think it's really worth asking.

00:08:34.070 --> 00:08:39.089
We want to understand the self in a deeper
philosophical societal level.

00:08:39.089 --> 00:08:45.130
Should we as a society care a whole lot about
how it's implemented in neurons or circuits

00:08:45.130 --> 00:08:47.340
of neurons?

00:08:47.340 --> 00:08:51.190
I'm interested in how that happens so for
me that's an easy answer.

00:08:51.190 --> 00:08:56.120
And I think media and the presence and at
these sorts of events here suggest that a

00:08:56.120 --> 00:09:00.250
lot of people are interested in those questions
but I think we should be very careful about

00:09:00.250 --> 00:09:05.510
how we assess neuro-scientific data and it's
relevant to these kind of societal questions.

00:09:05.510 --> 00:09:09.110
We also need to remember that neuroscience
is really in its infancy, especially this

00:09:09.110 --> 00:09:17.460
level of thinking about brain processes in
healthy human beings and how they are carried

00:09:17.460 --> 00:09:23.950
out on a very fine timescale like sitting
here and thinking about questions of the self.

00:09:23.950 --> 00:09:25.600
Those are the things we want to get at.

00:09:25.600 --> 00:09:27.670
But they're very hard to get at.

00:09:27.670 --> 00:09:31.030
I mean you asked about kind of how we can
even start doing that, what are some of the

00:09:31.030 --> 00:09:33.450
tools we have.

00:09:33.450 --> 00:09:40.320
And we have many, many of the most powerful
tools for understanding the brain.

00:09:40.320 --> 00:09:43.340
Historically did not exist in humans.

00:09:43.340 --> 00:09:48.510
We can debate and people do whether a sense
of self is a uniquely human phenomenon without

00:09:48.510 --> 00:09:52.590
even engaging in that debate I think we're
interested in that aspect of it.

00:09:52.590 --> 00:09:58.550
And that limits the tools we have to mostly
two kinds.

00:09:58.550 --> 00:10:02.370
And one is looking at patients with brain
damage.

00:10:02.370 --> 00:10:05.830
And what that does to their sense of self
and Nina and I were just discussing some of

00:10:05.830 --> 00:10:08.340
her work on this topic.

00:10:08.340 --> 00:10:11.360
And you know that I think that that that can
be useful.

00:10:11.360 --> 00:10:16.130
There are certainly cases of brain damage
impacting, changing people's sense of self.

00:10:16.130 --> 00:10:20.330
And again I think what that tells us is mostly
that it's not in the knee or the toe.

00:10:20.330 --> 00:10:21.630
It's in the brain.

00:10:21.630 --> 00:10:27.010
And then the other tool that I think has really
changed the public and scientific perception

00:10:27.010 --> 00:10:33.320
of the role of the brain in many complicated,
interesting phenomena, is human brain imaging.

00:10:33.320 --> 00:10:38.670
So we are now in a position and have been
for about 10 to 15 years with increasing power

00:10:38.670 --> 00:10:43.940
to put healthy human beings in a brain scanner
and measure where in their brain there is

00:10:43.940 --> 00:10:44.940
activity.

00:10:44.940 --> 00:10:48.740
None of us think that the self lives in a
particular node in the brain.

00:10:48.740 --> 00:10:52.680
So it's a challenge to come up with ways to
ask the right questions.

00:10:52.680 --> 00:10:54.710
Given that somewhat blunt tool.

00:10:54.710 --> 00:10:55.710
Yes.

00:10:55.710 --> 00:11:01.640
So do the experimental philosophers in the
crowd want to actually engage with that and

00:11:01.640 --> 00:11:05.170
say, well first of all what is experimental
philosophy?

00:11:05.170 --> 00:11:07.880
Let's ask that and then.

00:11:07.880 --> 00:11:10.470
Let's ask the author of the textbook.

00:11:10.470 --> 00:11:14.060
So experimental philosophy is an attempt to
go after these kinds of questions that we

00:11:14.060 --> 00:11:15.520
traditionally associate with philosophy.

00:11:15.520 --> 00:11:19.320
But using these kinds of methods, using systematic
experimental studies.

00:11:19.320 --> 00:11:24.110
So the kind of thing that Daphna was interested
in is the question, how is it that people

00:11:24.110 --> 00:11:28.170
actually engage in certain actions, what behaviors
and so forth?

00:11:28.170 --> 00:11:31.430
We could also ask how do people ordinarily
understand the self?

00:11:31.430 --> 00:11:34.460
How do people normally think about what it
is to be a self?

00:11:34.460 --> 00:11:36.340
How the self extends over time?

00:11:36.340 --> 00:11:40.050
How the self is related to our moral identity
and moral responsibility.

00:11:40.050 --> 00:11:42.150
What the core of the self is, what's important?

00:11:42.150 --> 00:11:44.930
And experimental philosophy involves the experimental
philosophy of the self.

00:11:44.930 --> 00:11:49.620
Involves the scientific study of that-the
scientific study of the concepts that people

00:11:49.620 --> 00:11:55.660
ordinarily use to understand what the self
is most fundamentally about.

00:11:55.660 --> 00:12:01.610
Just adding to that, one question you often
get if you do experimental work but you have

00:12:01.610 --> 00:12:08.530
an employer who is in the philosophy department,
you get a lot of resistance I think for people

00:12:08.530 --> 00:12:09.530
like us.

00:12:09.530 --> 00:12:10.530
We're really interested in questions.

00:12:10.530 --> 00:12:14.930
And if you're asking a question you should
never restrict yourself to a single methodology.

00:12:14.930 --> 00:12:18.120
For training reasons, it's very often useful
to specialize.

00:12:18.120 --> 00:12:22.840
But you should be open to the possibility
that multiple fields can contribute to answering

00:12:22.840 --> 00:12:23.840
the questions.

00:12:23.840 --> 00:12:28.040
If you're interested in the self, history
can contribute, literature can contribute,

00:12:28.040 --> 00:12:33.070
neuroscience can contribute, sociology, anthropology,
philosophy and psychology.

00:12:33.070 --> 00:12:38.060
No individual can take on all of those fields
so obviously it's going to be a interdisciplinary

00:12:38.060 --> 00:12:40.120
effort, a big conversation.

00:12:40.120 --> 00:12:45.210
But the more training you can get in the more
fields, the more resources you have as an

00:12:45.210 --> 00:12:47.790
individual to bring to bear on the topic question.

00:12:47.790 --> 00:12:51.890
I want to just give one example going back
to Daphna I mean I think with respect to the

00:12:51.890 --> 00:12:53.030
self.

00:12:53.030 --> 00:12:57.730
A very good case of where these fields have
come together is in some of the experimental

00:12:57.730 --> 00:13:01.920
work that I think Nina will talk about.

00:13:01.920 --> 00:13:07.240
We really have been finding that morality
is an important part of personal identity.

00:13:07.240 --> 00:13:08.530
But what is morality?

00:13:08.530 --> 00:13:09.779
Well sociology.

00:13:09.779 --> 00:13:15.800
All of those things contribute economics,
but neuroscience has helped us see that moral

00:13:15.800 --> 00:13:18.700
responses are connected to certain brain structures.

00:13:18.700 --> 00:13:22.790
One of the interesting things about those
brain structures is we know their job profile

00:13:22.790 --> 00:13:24.720
outside of the moral domain.

00:13:24.720 --> 00:13:26.890
And it seems like they do at least two other
things.

00:13:26.890 --> 00:13:30.470
One of them is they're essentially involved
in emotion.

00:13:30.470 --> 00:13:34.800
And another is these very same brain structures
some of them like the posterior cingulate,

00:13:34.800 --> 00:13:37.840
are also involved in perception of the body.

00:13:37.840 --> 00:13:42.390
So here we get this big social theory of the
self coming out of philosophical ideas supported

00:13:42.390 --> 00:13:47.260
by psychology, linking cells to morality and
then we get brain science, taking that to

00:13:47.260 --> 00:13:51.700
the next stage, linking morality to emotion,
linking emotion to the body and suddenly this

00:13:51.700 --> 00:13:57.260
very cultural thing, like moral values gets
grounded in the physical corpus in ways that

00:13:57.260 --> 00:14:02.310
we couldn't have really discovered I think
without the kinds of tools that Daphna uses.

00:14:02.310 --> 00:14:04.850
Well I want to follow up on that on the point
about emotions.

00:14:04.850 --> 00:14:10.060
I think it's very important but I'd actually
love for Nina to talk a little bit about her

00:14:10.060 --> 00:14:13.850
experience, because you were trained in psychology
right, in cognitive psychology and now you

00:14:13.850 --> 00:14:17.589
are working with the experimental philosophers.

00:14:17.589 --> 00:14:20.390
So how you know why did you decide to do that?

00:14:20.390 --> 00:14:25.590
And is it driven by the questions or by the
tools you know- how does that work together?

00:14:25.590 --> 00:14:28.870
The question is what brought me over to the
dark side.

00:14:28.870 --> 00:14:35.440
Well I've always been interested in philosophy
and you know even as an undergrad, I was doing

00:14:35.440 --> 00:14:40.170
work in cognitive science which draws a lot
from philosophy as well as psychology in the

00:14:40.170 --> 00:14:41.600
brain sciences.

00:14:41.600 --> 00:14:44.400
So I never really saw it as a trajectory to
the dark side.

00:14:44.400 --> 00:14:47.170
Maybe I always been embedded.

00:14:47.170 --> 00:14:52.760
And the decision to go to get my Ph.D. in
psychology was actually largely arbitrary.

00:14:52.760 --> 00:14:57.340
I thought maybe there was a marginal, marginally
better chance I would get a job at the end

00:14:57.340 --> 00:14:58.630
of it but it wasn't.

00:14:58.630 --> 00:15:02.680
I didn't feel like you know I defected at
any point.

00:15:02.680 --> 00:15:07.540
So working with philosophers, especially empirically
minded philosophers who care about the experimental

00:15:07.540 --> 00:15:13.500
work the scientific work, feels very natural
and it's been very productive as well.

00:15:13.500 --> 00:15:17.020
So your sense of self is a very practical
one.

00:15:17.020 --> 00:15:19.660
But Jesse, I'd love to.

00:15:19.660 --> 00:15:24.970
I'd love to actually follow up on this concept
of emotion and the importance of emotion in

00:15:24.970 --> 00:15:29.270
the self because I think it's actually a thread
that runs through all of your work to some

00:15:29.270 --> 00:15:33.970
extent because we have you know emotional
memories, and we have emotions, and how they're

00:15:33.970 --> 00:15:34.970
impacted.

00:15:34.970 --> 00:15:38.500
You mentioned briefly you know Nina's work
on disease and we'll talk a little bit about

00:15:38.500 --> 00:15:40.070
that.

00:15:40.070 --> 00:15:41.070
Values and moralities.

00:15:41.070 --> 00:15:47.550
So I'd love to get your perspective on what
the role of emotions is when it comes to defining

00:15:47.550 --> 00:15:49.899
what self is.

00:15:49.899 --> 00:15:54.880
Well I mean I think we are phrases like, "I
don't feel myself today" and if you stop and

00:15:54.880 --> 00:15:58.570
pause for a minute the idea of feeling like
a self, what does that even mean?

00:15:58.570 --> 00:16:00.700
How is the self something that you can feel?

00:16:00.700 --> 00:16:05.440
And I think the insight that's captured in
those kinds of clichés is that we really

00:16:05.440 --> 00:16:11.190
can in a sort of deep introspective way, recognize
when we're acting in accord with how we normally

00:16:11.190 --> 00:16:12.190
act.

00:16:12.190 --> 00:16:13.190
And when we depart from that.

00:16:13.190 --> 00:16:17.930
And to not feel yourself can be in part emoting
differently, feeling differently.

00:16:17.930 --> 00:16:21.130
Things that once stirred you now leave you
apathetic.

00:16:21.130 --> 00:16:26.040
Those who have experienced kind of anecdotal
symptoms associated with depression who were

00:16:26.040 --> 00:16:30.910
you know had sources of joy who they try to
self-medicate by doing something that they've

00:16:30.910 --> 00:16:35.700
found worthwhile in other areas of their live
and find themselves indifferent, are really

00:16:35.700 --> 00:16:38.980
discovering a kind of rupture of identity.

00:16:38.980 --> 00:16:43.740
So I think in that way we can find a lot of
evidence that emotions are central to what

00:16:43.740 --> 00:16:46.630
it is to experience yourself as being the
same person.

00:16:46.630 --> 00:16:50.450
How you react emotionally is central to who
you are.

00:16:50.450 --> 00:16:56.200
So who would you be a different person if
you suddenly descended into clinical depression?

00:16:56.200 --> 00:17:02.320
I think the answer is with Josh's original
comment that the self has many facets and

00:17:02.320 --> 00:17:05.419
there is no one thing that is the self.

00:17:05.419 --> 00:17:09.900
But I do think when we deal with mental illness,
one of the challenges for people who suffer

00:17:09.900 --> 00:17:15.669
it and for family members too is that a lot
of these symptoms are really insults to the

00:17:15.669 --> 00:17:17.970
self in ways that need to be recognized.

00:17:17.970 --> 00:17:21.600
Depression isn't just feeling down or feeling
incapacitated.

00:17:21.600 --> 00:17:26.759
It really is a much more radical shift from
your mode of being.

00:17:26.759 --> 00:17:31.440
Another aspect of this is depression is an
emotional style and a lot of us I think who

00:17:31.440 --> 00:17:37.279
have sort of symptoms of this kind of emotional
symptoms begin to identify with those symptoms.

00:17:37.279 --> 00:17:42.649
So why are so many people reluctant to give
up on their depression or medicate their depression

00:17:42.649 --> 00:17:43.980
or get over their depression.

00:17:43.980 --> 00:17:46.919
Why do those phrases meet with some resistance?

00:17:46.919 --> 00:17:52.639
Part of it is your emotional mode is core
to identity in a way that is threatened by

00:17:52.639 --> 00:17:56.590
significant changes even in the positive clinical
directions.

00:17:56.590 --> 00:18:01.440
Do any of you guys want to jump in?

00:18:01.440 --> 00:18:06.999
I mean I may have found the thing that I disagree
with Jesse about.

00:18:06.999 --> 00:18:10.610
Maybe when we talk about a moral determinate,
there is actually no disagreement.

00:18:10.610 --> 00:18:14.779
But there is a difference between feeling
like yourself and this question of what the

00:18:14.779 --> 00:18:17.140
self feels like from the inside.

00:18:17.140 --> 00:18:19.860
And what would make someone else seem like
a different person.

00:18:19.860 --> 00:18:22.350
And those two things can come apart.

00:18:22.350 --> 00:18:28.520
So in some of my work on patients with different
brain diseases, including depression, depressive

00:18:28.520 --> 00:18:31.789
symptoms are quite common with neurodegenerative
diseases.

00:18:31.789 --> 00:18:37.950
We find that the extent to which someone is
experiencing or rather is displaying the symptoms

00:18:37.950 --> 00:18:43.720
of depression is-doesn't at all predict whether
someone says this is the same person or I

00:18:43.720 --> 00:18:45.710
feel like I don't recognize them anymore.

00:18:45.710 --> 00:18:46.710
So.

00:18:46.710 --> 00:18:52.039
And that was true for other emotional states,
happiness sadness.

00:18:52.039 --> 00:18:56.230
So it doesn't seem like emotion is playing
a very big role in determining the identity

00:18:56.230 --> 00:18:58.869
of other people, their continuous identity.

00:18:58.869 --> 00:19:04.980
Although you probably are right that depression
from the inside really changes how we how

00:19:04.980 --> 00:19:06.510
we feel like whether we feel like ourselves.

00:19:06.510 --> 00:19:07.750
So actually yeah, please.

00:19:07.750 --> 00:19:11.529
So maybe just building on the kind of thing,
you know we're saying.

00:19:11.529 --> 00:19:15.429
So traditionally philosophers often suggest
that ultimately the true nature of the self,

00:19:15.429 --> 00:19:18.519
the real essence of who we are is our capacity
to reason.

00:19:18.519 --> 00:19:22.080
So the thought was that our emotions are just
kind of getting in the way of the self.

00:19:22.080 --> 00:19:25.510
If we could only get rid of those emotions
then we'll be able to express who we really

00:19:25.510 --> 00:19:27.090
most fundamentally are.

00:19:27.090 --> 00:19:30.730
So people will give these examples that are
in many ways I feel seem compelling.

00:19:30.730 --> 00:19:35.830
So consider for example someone who is a heroin
addict and when he reasons, he thinks, I have

00:19:35.830 --> 00:19:37.590
to kick this, I have to get clean.

00:19:37.590 --> 00:19:39.960
But his emotions are driving him to get another
hit.

00:19:39.960 --> 00:19:44.630
In this case we would say, oh his true self,
who he really is, is something about his ability

00:19:44.630 --> 00:19:45.710
to reason.

00:19:45.710 --> 00:19:50.159
It's his- that part of him is strong and toward
getting clean and it's who he really is.

00:19:50.159 --> 00:19:52.809
This other thing, his emotions are drawing
him toward.

00:19:52.809 --> 00:19:53.899
That's not who he really is.

00:19:53.899 --> 00:19:57.499
But I think what we're seeing in a lot of
recent work coming out from Jesse, from Nina,

00:19:57.499 --> 00:20:01.850
from many others is that this intuition we
have has actually has nothing to do with the

00:20:01.850 --> 00:20:03.700
difference between reason and emotion.

00:20:03.700 --> 00:20:07.529
Rather it just has to do with the fact that
we think that heroin is bad and that getting

00:20:07.529 --> 00:20:10.419
a-kicking heroin is good.

00:20:10.419 --> 00:20:14.690
And I think you could see it really easily
if you just imagined taking the reason and

00:20:14.690 --> 00:20:16.879
the emotion and just sort of switching the
roles.

00:20:16.879 --> 00:20:21.739
So I suppose there was a high school student
who on reflection thought, I should really

00:20:21.739 --> 00:20:22.739
just start doing heroin.

00:20:22.739 --> 00:20:26.821
It will increase my social status of being
more popular in school, is going to mean a

00:20:26.821 --> 00:20:27.909
really good direction.

00:20:27.909 --> 00:20:32.090
But then when she thinks about doing this,
she just has this emotion that draws her to

00:20:32.090 --> 00:20:33.090
not do it.

00:20:33.090 --> 00:20:34.090
She just kind of feels icky about it.

00:20:34.090 --> 00:20:36.049
It just sort of feels wrong to her.

00:20:36.049 --> 00:20:40.610
So this sort of states that she has are just
the inverse of the heroin addict that appears

00:20:40.610 --> 00:20:41.950
in the traditional philosophical example.

00:20:41.950 --> 00:20:44.370
And here I think we would say exactly the
opposite.

00:20:44.370 --> 00:20:48.700
We would say, that voice within her, that
feeling of icky-ness or discomfort with doing

00:20:48.700 --> 00:20:49.700
heroin.

00:20:49.700 --> 00:20:53.609
That's her true self and her capacity to reason
which is telling her to ignore that voice.

00:20:53.609 --> 00:20:56.299
That's something she has to get rid of to
get back in touch with who she really is.

00:20:56.299 --> 00:20:58.029
So I'd love to.

00:20:58.029 --> 00:21:02.250
I can I completely agree and I was thinking
of making a similar point as it emerges from

00:21:02.250 --> 00:21:07.480
the neuroscience kind of point of view which
is that we share as humans this intuition

00:21:07.480 --> 00:21:11.100
of sort of say, your cognition versus emotion.

00:21:11.100 --> 00:21:17.590
One of the really interesting conclusions
from our work on the brain over the past decade

00:21:17.590 --> 00:21:23.169
and more is that the brain doesn't respect
those boundaries at all nor really should

00:21:23.169 --> 00:21:28.029
it for the reasons you mentioned and so the
idea of thinking of feelings as being separate

00:21:28.029 --> 00:21:31.289
from thinking doesn't make sense.

00:21:31.289 --> 00:21:36.950
We don't have emotional and kind of rational
parts in our brain that are dueling it out

00:21:36.950 --> 00:21:43.289
even though that idea is very compelling and
has been around for a very long time, back

00:21:43.289 --> 00:21:45.919
to Aristotle I believe.

00:21:45.919 --> 00:21:49.230
Looking at the philosophers for approval.

00:21:49.230 --> 00:21:54.419
And so in many ways, it kind of changes the
question we want to ask then is not what is-where

00:21:54.419 --> 00:21:58.409
in the brain do these two forces live and
where do they duke it out?

00:21:58.409 --> 00:22:04.570
But what are the very specific circuits in
common that they take advantage of and what

00:22:04.570 --> 00:22:06.659
are the principles that drive them?

00:22:06.659 --> 00:22:11.350
Because the fact is still at the level of
what we do our actions, which I think really

00:22:11.350 --> 00:22:16.710
is kind of how the most conservative or useful
way to think the self in many ways.

00:22:16.710 --> 00:22:19.320
When we do something we take one action.

00:22:19.320 --> 00:22:21.789
We either go for the heroin or not.

00:22:21.789 --> 00:22:24.799
We either eat the chocolate cake or resist
it.

00:22:24.799 --> 00:22:30.049
And so one of the things we've been really
interested in is understanding not how emotional

00:22:30.049 --> 00:22:37.950
loses or wins but what are the circuits that
bias our behavior to reach for something or

00:22:37.950 --> 00:22:38.950
not reach for it.

00:22:38.950 --> 00:22:42.220
That bias our decision to do something or
not do it.

00:22:42.220 --> 00:22:44.249
So it's interesting that there's such convergence.

00:22:44.249 --> 00:22:46.639
I wasn't aware of it.

00:22:46.639 --> 00:22:51.669
Well I think it's also it's an interesting
question that Josh keeps bringing up of your

00:22:51.669 --> 00:22:57.659
quote unquote true self versus kind of your
other self which isn't your true self.

00:22:57.659 --> 00:23:02.929
And I'd love for you to talk a little bit
more about that and also how much of that

00:23:02.929 --> 00:23:09.519
is actually imposed externally versus how
you really feel about yourself.

00:23:09.519 --> 00:23:15.899
So if we give the example of the heroin addict,
does she think that she has a true self and

00:23:15.899 --> 00:23:20.149
something that's not a true self or is that
just a judgment that we're making about her?

00:23:20.149 --> 00:23:25.170
Well I was going to introduce this topic so
maybe I should hear from some of the other

00:23:25.170 --> 00:23:26.170
folks.

00:23:26.170 --> 00:23:27.170
Well you're the expert on the true self.

00:23:27.170 --> 00:23:31.970
To what extent this kind of thing is this
introduced externally versus something that

00:23:31.970 --> 00:23:33.509
appears within our selves?

00:23:33.509 --> 00:23:38.149
It seems like initially almost all research
on this notion of the true self was based

00:23:38.149 --> 00:23:40.330
on how people think about their own true selves.

00:23:40.330 --> 00:23:44.710
And this research would ask Nina about Nina's
true self, Jesse about Jesse's true self,

00:23:44.710 --> 00:23:46.669
and Daphna about Daphna's true self.

00:23:46.669 --> 00:23:50.789
What this kind of research finds is that people
always thought that their true self is something

00:23:50.789 --> 00:23:54.539
really awesome, fantastic, just absolutely
wonderful in every way.

00:23:54.539 --> 00:23:57.970
So the more you feel like you're in touch
with your true self, the more you feel that

00:23:57.970 --> 00:24:02.239
your life is a meaningful one, the more you
feel that your decisions are on target.

00:24:02.239 --> 00:24:04.960
The more you feel like you're doing something
that you're supposed to do.

00:24:04.960 --> 00:24:07.100
And it's very easy to dismiss that kind of
result.

00:24:07.100 --> 00:24:11.129
You just think, the reason people think so
well of their own true selves is because they

00:24:11.129 --> 00:24:13.600
think so well about everything about themselves.

00:24:13.600 --> 00:24:17.409
Everyone thinks that they're the best driver,
that they're the easiest to get along with,

00:24:17.409 --> 00:24:21.139
almost every single professor at a university
thinks they're above average.

00:24:21.139 --> 00:24:24.490
So maybe in just same way people think that
their true self is the really wonderful kind

00:24:24.490 --> 00:24:25.720
of true self.

00:24:25.720 --> 00:24:29.350
But just in the past few years, people have
been trying this in a different way where

00:24:29.350 --> 00:24:31.899
they ask people about the true selves of other
people.

00:24:31.899 --> 00:24:36.049
So this time now we ask Nina about Daphna's
true self, Daphna about Jesse's true self,

00:24:36.049 --> 00:24:37.379
and Jesse about Nina's true self.

00:24:37.379 --> 00:24:42.029
What you find then, is this really extreme
tendency whereby people also think that other

00:24:42.029 --> 00:24:43.499
people's true selves are good.

00:24:43.499 --> 00:24:47.289
In fact people think not only the true selves
of people they like are good, but that the

00:24:47.289 --> 00:24:49.830
true selves of the people they most hate are
good.

00:24:49.830 --> 00:24:54.759
Even the most liberal person will think, there's
something within George W. Bush calling him,

00:24:54.759 --> 00:25:00.619
his true self speaking to him and saying what
you're doing in Guantanamo is morally wrong.

00:25:00.619 --> 00:25:04.100
Deep down it's not what he really is really
being drawn to do.

00:25:04.100 --> 00:25:09.559
So there seems to be something about human
beings that makes them think of themselves

00:25:09.559 --> 00:25:11.600
as being-having a good true self.

00:25:11.600 --> 00:25:13.929
But it's not something about it being your
own self.

00:25:13.929 --> 00:25:19.159
It's something that applies equally to how
everyone else will think about your true self.

00:25:19.159 --> 00:25:25.809
So it seems like there's some moral judgment
actually that is that is being imposed from

00:25:25.809 --> 00:25:30.809
the outside, which is defining true selves
for other people.

00:25:30.809 --> 00:25:35.310
So I might think that you know the true self
of someone else is good for a different reason

00:25:35.310 --> 00:25:37.659
than you.

00:25:37.659 --> 00:25:40.749
We might actually have it backwards.

00:25:40.749 --> 00:25:46.289
Absolutely and that actually I mean Josh is
too modest to refer to his own work directly

00:25:46.289 --> 00:25:50.701
when he's describing this that he has this
wonderful study where he shows that if you

00:25:50.701 --> 00:25:59.049
ask, if you describe this-a case of someone
who is-has homosexual feelings but since he

00:25:59.049 --> 00:26:04.879
is highly religious and his religion prescribes
homosexuality he believes that he shouldn't

00:26:04.879 --> 00:26:10.220
be gay and then you ask people, which is his
true self?

00:26:10.220 --> 00:26:12.919
The drive to be gay that's emotional.

00:26:12.919 --> 00:26:16.090
Or the intellectual belief that he ought not
to be gay?

00:26:16.090 --> 00:26:19.989
And it turns out that it depends entirely
on whether you ask liberals or conservatives

00:26:19.989 --> 00:26:21.149
this question.

00:26:21.149 --> 00:26:24.300
So if you yourself believe that it's wrong
to be gay, you say his true self is the part

00:26:24.300 --> 00:26:26.340
of telling him not to be gay.

00:26:26.340 --> 00:26:29.729
But if you think that it's it's actually a
non-moral issue whether you're gay or not.

00:26:29.729 --> 00:26:33.809
Then you say, yeah, his true self are those
emotional drives.

00:26:33.809 --> 00:26:40.370
So this actually gets to some of Jesse's work
about the self as being a socially determined

00:26:40.370 --> 00:26:41.370
thing.

00:26:41.370 --> 00:26:46.429
Can you talk a little bit about kind of that
conception of ourselves coming from our experiences

00:26:46.429 --> 00:26:47.659
and socialization.

00:26:47.659 --> 00:26:52.269
And I'm was also wondering you know if you
are someone who was raised without a lot of

00:26:52.269 --> 00:26:56.340
people around, would you develop a sense of
self in the same way?

00:26:56.340 --> 00:26:57.340
Great.

00:26:57.340 --> 00:27:02.090
You know I just we have to come back briefly
to Joshua's example of the attribution of

00:27:02.090 --> 00:27:03.850
goodness to moral self.

00:27:03.850 --> 00:27:09.539
When I recently reread Hannah Arendt's Eichmann
in Jerusalem, which is a very provocative

00:27:09.539 --> 00:27:10.869
I think, very problematic book.

00:27:10.869 --> 00:27:15.899
But one of the things that really offended
people about that book when she first published

00:27:15.899 --> 00:27:21.299
it is the suggestion that Adolf Eichmann was
banal, that there was some way in which he

00:27:21.299 --> 00:27:26.950
was just an ordinary bureaucrat who was co-opted
into this diabolical project.

00:27:26.950 --> 00:27:32.710
Deprive people of their desire to blame him
by seeing him as an evil self in her early

00:27:32.710 --> 00:27:33.710
work on totalitarianism.

00:27:33.710 --> 00:27:36.679
She had this idea of radical evil.

00:27:36.679 --> 00:27:40.720
Some people are just at their deepest core
bad.

00:27:40.720 --> 00:27:44.389
And I think the flip side, the counterpoint
to the general phenomenon you're describing

00:27:44.389 --> 00:27:49.509
is in certain contexts, we really do want
to hold people accountable at the core.

00:27:49.509 --> 00:27:53.739
And recent work on Eichmann in particular
has come out suggesting that he was very systematic

00:27:53.739 --> 00:27:58.559
and non-apologetic Nazi ideologue, which helped
confirm this idea that it wasn't just that

00:27:58.559 --> 00:28:02.970
he was pushed around like in the Stanley Milgram
experiment by circumstance but that there

00:28:02.970 --> 00:28:07.659
was a driving motivation to engage in the
acts that he perpetrated.

00:28:07.659 --> 00:28:13.210
Which of course, that is a social dimension
in the self because it's how we think about

00:28:13.210 --> 00:28:18.139
other people's selves, what we want to project
when we talk about ourselves on Facebook and

00:28:18.139 --> 00:28:22.769
social networks, we're listing these traits
and we try to list things about ourselves

00:28:22.769 --> 00:28:26.059
that will ingratiate us, but also help us
form social groups.

00:28:26.059 --> 00:28:31.549
I just was refereeing a paper showing that
if there's a transformation from being a punk

00:28:31.549 --> 00:28:33.679
rocker to being a jock.

00:28:33.679 --> 00:28:37.090
That's considered a sort of a bad thing.

00:28:37.090 --> 00:28:41.649
And that's a sort of a more radical loss of
self than if you go from being a- sorry, punk

00:28:41.649 --> 00:28:43.570
rock to jock a good thing.

00:28:43.570 --> 00:28:47.470
It's seen as a moral improvement.

00:28:47.470 --> 00:28:53.600
If you go from being a jock to a punk rocker,
that's seen as some sort of degeneration.

00:28:53.600 --> 00:28:56.399
So we have these things we want to project
to others about social identity.

00:28:56.399 --> 00:29:00.099
If you tell somebody you're a punk rocker,
they're immediately going to form all kinds

00:29:00.099 --> 00:29:01.869
of associations with you.

00:29:01.869 --> 00:29:08.100
But one of the things that happens in these
group membership identities is we form affiliations,

00:29:08.100 --> 00:29:09.849
we find like minded people.

00:29:09.849 --> 00:29:13.120
So why is it so important on Facebook to say
your political values.

00:29:13.120 --> 00:29:16.230
And the answer is you don't want to have any
contact with people who are your political

00:29:16.230 --> 00:29:17.940
opposites.

00:29:17.940 --> 00:29:22.679
So if you find out the person you're dating
and we have some data on this, belongs to

00:29:22.679 --> 00:29:23.879
a different political party.

00:29:23.879 --> 00:29:28.799
Even now with like Bernie and Hillary, I've
lost friendships over you know who-which candidate

00:29:28.799 --> 00:29:33.929
you support for the Democratic nomination
and that that divide makes it impossible to

00:29:33.929 --> 00:29:35.500
form close affiliation.

00:29:35.500 --> 00:29:39.179
So when you say, "I'm a Hillary person" or
"I'm a Bernie person," you're saying something

00:29:39.179 --> 00:29:44.480
about your identity that bears on who can
be your friend and that that I think is a

00:29:44.480 --> 00:29:50.779
deep revelation that the self is not about
me, qua individual, it's about me qua group.

00:29:50.779 --> 00:29:56.279
Who are the people who are going to fortify
me in life as part of my extended identity.

00:29:56.279 --> 00:29:59.970
So
wouldn't that depend though, on how important

00:29:59.970 --> 00:30:02.960
politics were to you personally?

00:30:02.960 --> 00:30:08.340
So doesn't it actually depend on the person
and the relative importance of different things

00:30:08.340 --> 00:30:09.399
to your sense of self?

00:30:09.399 --> 00:30:14.090
So if politics is totally unimportant to me,
I'm not going to lose any friendships over

00:30:14.090 --> 00:30:16.220
Bernie supporters.

00:30:16.220 --> 00:30:17.220
We thought so.

00:30:17.220 --> 00:30:21.970
So with a collaborator, Javier Gomez-Lavin,
we did a series of studies where we gave people

00:30:21.970 --> 00:30:24.399
all kinds of traits that might matter to identity.

00:30:24.399 --> 00:30:30.059
Occupation, are you urban versus rural, what
are your pastimes, what are your music tastes,

00:30:30.059 --> 00:30:31.059
and so on.

00:30:31.059 --> 00:30:36.519
Among these we also had politics and religion,
which are very moral dimensions.

00:30:36.519 --> 00:30:40.019
And then we ask people how important are these
things to you?

00:30:40.019 --> 00:30:41.499
That was one question.

00:30:41.499 --> 00:30:45.299
And then if these things change, would you
still be the same person?

00:30:45.299 --> 00:30:49.059
For things like musical taste, people tended
to get very high ratings of importance.

00:30:49.059 --> 00:30:50.380
Music really matters to me.

00:30:50.380 --> 00:30:54.720
Or being urban, or my job really matters to
me, occupation is extremely important to most

00:30:54.720 --> 00:30:55.860
people.

00:30:55.860 --> 00:30:59.399
But when those things change would you still
be the same person if you change jobs?

00:30:59.399 --> 00:31:01.039
If you moved to a small town?

00:31:01.039 --> 00:31:02.039
Sure.

00:31:02.039 --> 00:31:04.320
Yeah that wouldn't threaten my identity.

00:31:04.320 --> 00:31:08.249
Would you be the same person if you change
your political party affiliation?

00:31:08.249 --> 00:31:12.320
Would you be the same person if you went to
a different church or adopted maybe went from

00:31:12.320 --> 00:31:15.099
being an atheist to a theist?

00:31:15.099 --> 00:31:16.099
People say yes.

00:31:16.099 --> 00:31:20.100
And strikingly in response to your question,
they say that is a threat to identity or a

00:31:20.100 --> 00:31:24.999
change to identity even if in the first question,
they say it isn't very important to them.

00:31:24.999 --> 00:31:29.629
So if you find somebody that-Nina has some
data that go in a slightly different direction

00:31:29.629 --> 00:31:31.690
on this that are very interesting.

00:31:31.690 --> 00:31:34.749
If you find people who say morality doesn't
matter much to me or politics don't matter

00:31:34.749 --> 00:31:35.749
much to me.

00:31:35.749 --> 00:31:39.909
And say, Ok, so if the next election cycle,
what if you've started voting for the other

00:31:39.909 --> 00:31:43.789
side or what have you started really caring
about politics all of a sudden?

00:31:43.789 --> 00:31:44.990
Would you be the same?

00:31:44.990 --> 00:31:46.390
And people tend to say no.

00:31:46.390 --> 00:31:48.039
Nina, do you want to...

00:31:48.039 --> 00:31:49.039
Yes.

00:31:49.039 --> 00:31:52.679
So we have this data that shows that.

00:31:52.679 --> 00:31:58.539
So first of all, we find that the part of
the self that's the most important to saying

00:31:58.539 --> 00:32:02.659
that someone is the same person with if you
look at different parts of the mind.

00:32:02.659 --> 00:32:08.210
So your memories, your moral capacities, your
personality, your preferences, the things

00:32:08.210 --> 00:32:11.970
you like and dislike, basic cognitive capacities.

00:32:11.970 --> 00:32:17.340
The factor that matters the most and all the
other factors, they matter a little bit.

00:32:17.340 --> 00:32:22.770
The one that matters the most are your moral
values, your moral beliefs, your moral actions.

00:32:22.770 --> 00:32:28.450
But if you go to psychopaths, and we have
this data, we say we give people a psychopathy

00:32:28.450 --> 00:32:33.679
test, and actually-so you can have someone
who is a clinical psychopath, you can also

00:32:33.679 --> 00:32:39.419
have-so everyone in this room would score
slightly different on the psychopathy test.

00:32:39.419 --> 00:32:42.269
Presumably there's no one out here who is
like a perfect psychopath.

00:32:42.269 --> 00:32:45.340
But there are some natural-
Well it's 1 in 100

00:32:45.340 --> 00:32:52.099
So there might be like a perfect 40 in the
audience right now.

00:32:52.099 --> 00:32:56.450
But for the most part, there is there's natural
variation.

00:32:56.450 --> 00:33:01.330
And people you know who are sort of high but
are subclinical might you know, go into business

00:33:01.330 --> 00:33:04.580
or something.

00:33:04.580 --> 00:33:11.219
But if you go to these people and you give
them the same sorts of battery of tests, they

00:33:11.219 --> 00:33:14.639
no longer show a preference for moral traits.

00:33:14.639 --> 00:33:18.489
Which goes against the suggestion that, oh
it's really about maneuvering through the

00:33:18.489 --> 00:33:20.379
social world.

00:33:20.379 --> 00:33:24.139
Because surely it's very important to psychopaths
to know you know who can they manipulate,

00:33:24.139 --> 00:33:26.759
who's gullible and trusting, and so forth.

00:33:26.759 --> 00:33:30.809
It really seems like what's going on is they
say, well this is what's important to me.

00:33:30.809 --> 00:33:33.239
"Are these moral traits important to me?

00:33:33.239 --> 00:33:34.730
No not really I don't value them.

00:33:34.730 --> 00:33:38.639
Therefore they're not really important to
the identity of other people either."

00:33:38.639 --> 00:33:39.639
I'd love to have...

00:33:39.639 --> 00:33:45.210
I actually would like to make a comment and-am
I allowed to ask a question?

00:33:45.210 --> 00:33:46.590
Yeah, please.

00:33:46.590 --> 00:33:52.869
So the comment is that in studies of things
like decision making and choices.

00:33:52.869 --> 00:33:57.039
So again I'm-I keep emphasizing like the actions
people take.

00:33:57.039 --> 00:34:02.359
There's a lot of data showing that if you
ask people to choose about say between two

00:34:02.359 --> 00:34:05.690
options, either for reward like between a
blue square and a red square and they're going

00:34:05.690 --> 00:34:10.200
to get money and they do this 200 times and
as the experimenter, you're controlling how

00:34:10.200 --> 00:34:13.070
likely they are to win on any given choice.

00:34:13.070 --> 00:34:16.869
People readily engage in this, especially
undergraduates who are excited about getting

00:34:16.869 --> 00:34:17.950
the money.

00:34:17.950 --> 00:34:23.399
And if you ask them later what they did, they
will come up with a story about how they made

00:34:23.399 --> 00:34:24.710
their choices.

00:34:24.710 --> 00:34:28.099
But if you look at what they actually did,
it doesn't fit their story.

00:34:28.099 --> 00:34:34.869
It fits often a story we have about how the
brain should be driving the actions.

00:34:34.869 --> 00:34:39.020
And so I've really come over the years, and
many of my colleagues, to feel like asking

00:34:39.020 --> 00:34:45.079
people what they think only tells you what
they think and doesn't necessarily get why

00:34:45.079 --> 00:34:46.399
they're doing what they're doing.

00:34:46.399 --> 00:34:54.961
And I guess that's a long lead to the question
of, and I ask it kind of naively, do you take

00:34:54.961 --> 00:34:59.839
for granted that asking people what they think
makes a self is actually, that those answers

00:34:59.839 --> 00:35:02.890
actually tell us what makes a self a self?

00:35:02.890 --> 00:35:05.080
Absolutely not.

00:35:05.080 --> 00:35:08.660
I think that these, these two are they're
distinct.

00:35:08.660 --> 00:35:14.140
But at the same time, the question of what
actually makes a self a self, although it's

00:35:14.140 --> 00:35:19.770
a metaphysical question in many ways, it's
not a question that probably could be answered

00:35:19.770 --> 00:35:24.980
satisfactorily with any sorts of studies.

00:35:24.980 --> 00:35:29.849
I mean I just I agree with Nina and I would
put forth your question but I would say as

00:35:29.849 --> 00:35:34.020
a matter of descriptive fact, morality has
this weird kind of tenacity.

00:35:34.020 --> 00:35:36.970
There's work by Linda Skitka on what is called
attitude strength.

00:35:36.970 --> 00:35:39.730
And it turns out, psychologists measure this
in different ways.

00:35:39.730 --> 00:35:41.779
How intensely do you feel about something?

00:35:41.779 --> 00:35:43.770
How important is it to you?

00:35:43.770 --> 00:35:49.820
And if it were to change, would it be a kind
of loss, how connected is it to your other

00:35:49.820 --> 00:35:51.920
core, stabilizing values?

00:35:51.920 --> 00:35:57.450
She found that morality was the only thing
that showed up as correlated across all measures

00:35:57.450 --> 00:35:58.940
of attitude and strength very consistently.

00:35:58.940 --> 00:36:05.829
There are also studies of political party
affiliation in the US over the lifespan.

00:36:05.829 --> 00:36:12.200
Within a 40 year span, the variation is about
six people remain the same to about the degree

00:36:12.200 --> 00:36:13.200
of 65 percent.

00:36:13.200 --> 00:36:19.119
So within basically a whole voting life from
your earliest elections to you know older

00:36:19.119 --> 00:36:23.299
ages, you're voting pretty similarly, despite
dramatic changes that might be taking place

00:36:23.299 --> 00:36:24.539
in your society.

00:36:24.539 --> 00:36:28.119
If you look at 10 year periods, it's about
80 percent.

00:36:28.119 --> 00:36:32.000
Take the big five personality traits, the
most validated traits of personality that

00:36:32.000 --> 00:36:33.520
have ever been studied.

00:36:33.520 --> 00:36:39.140
Openness, conscientiousness, extroversion,
neuroticism and agreeableness.

00:36:39.140 --> 00:36:40.210
Those are less stable.

00:36:40.210 --> 00:36:46.680
They're very stable, but you can look at say
5, 10 year intervals and in some studies,

00:36:46.680 --> 00:36:51.609
the agreement over those times is again in
the 60 something percentile for fairly short

00:36:51.609 --> 00:36:52.609
periods.

00:36:52.609 --> 00:36:58.630
Over the lifespan, that can drop off precipitously
and very susceptible to dramatic life changes.

00:36:58.630 --> 00:37:02.880
Political values, which is an indirect sort
of measure of moral attitudes, do show a kind

00:37:02.880 --> 00:37:07.020
of tenacity that I think is unusual among
our traits.

00:37:07.020 --> 00:37:13.539
So one of the things that I'd love for Daphna
to talk about since I think the three of you

00:37:13.539 --> 00:37:16.870
have been emphasizing morality as kind of
the most important thing.

00:37:16.870 --> 00:37:20.140
And Daphna obviously studies memory.

00:37:20.140 --> 00:37:25.240
And I'm curious about your thoughts on whether
memory might be more important than morality

00:37:25.240 --> 00:37:27.300
to us?

00:37:27.300 --> 00:37:32.671
Well I would have to put it as a competition
between them.

00:37:32.671 --> 00:37:39.741
I think for me somebody who studies memory,
I think the point I'd want to make in the

00:37:39.741 --> 00:37:45.279
allotted time is that memory is much more
fundamental to our behaviors in general than

00:37:45.279 --> 00:37:46.279
we think.

00:37:46.279 --> 00:37:49.220
It's not just our ability to remember something
we did yesterday.

00:37:49.220 --> 00:37:55.660
But is that many of our actions if not every
single one is informed by past experience,

00:37:55.660 --> 00:37:57.869
one way or another.

00:37:57.869 --> 00:38:04.950
And I think that's a very interesting an interesting
challenge is to understand how that emerges.

00:38:04.950 --> 00:38:12.390
It makes sense for a brain and a being to
take advantage of our past experiences and

00:38:12.390 --> 00:38:17.640
use them and so the key question I'm interested
in is exactly how that happens.

00:38:17.640 --> 00:38:24.020
So I think memory is fundamental to many aspects
of our behaviors, including our sense of self.

00:38:24.020 --> 00:38:31.170
And one of the biggest questions then is how
as for us as neuroscientists where we're just

00:38:31.170 --> 00:38:32.329
in many ways starting out.

00:38:32.329 --> 00:38:39.010
We know a pretty solid amount about the likelihood
for any one of us at any given moment that

00:38:39.010 --> 00:38:41.059
will remember a particular event.

00:38:41.059 --> 00:38:46.640
OK so we know a lot about how an individual
memory is formed and then retrieved.

00:38:46.640 --> 00:38:52.350
I think the interesting intersection between
memory and sense of self starts when we try

00:38:52.350 --> 00:38:55.140
to understand how those memories get connected
over time.

00:38:55.140 --> 00:39:01.410
And we can think from the memory angle on
the self as a self as being sort of emerging

00:39:01.410 --> 00:39:06.470
as a interrelated with the memories as they
unfold over a lifetime.

00:39:06.470 --> 00:39:10.140
So we're you know as a scientist of the brain,
we now can say what happens with a single

00:39:10.140 --> 00:39:11.960
memory, maybe with two or three memories.

00:39:11.960 --> 00:39:17.910
And we know that a big job that the parts
of our brain that form memories, one of the

00:39:17.910 --> 00:39:22.900
big challenges is that they are constantly
weaving our memories into a network.

00:39:22.900 --> 00:39:28.150
We'd like to think that that network is on
one hand gives rise to a sense of self, but

00:39:28.150 --> 00:39:33.760
we also know and I think it's important to
emphasize the other direction, that we are-our

00:39:33.760 --> 00:39:35.450
sense of expectations about the world.

00:39:35.450 --> 00:39:40.470
I don't know if you count that as self, but
there the way we perceive the world will also

00:39:40.470 --> 00:39:42.599
change the way our memories are formed.

00:39:42.599 --> 00:39:45.109
So memories are very personal in that sense.

00:39:45.109 --> 00:39:49.670
They're absolutely not just a veridical record
of what happened in a given moment or our

00:39:49.670 --> 00:39:50.670
lives.

00:39:50.670 --> 00:39:54.520
Their influenced heavily by what we expected
would happen and those expectations are shaped

00:39:54.520 --> 00:39:56.560
by the memories we had before them.

00:39:56.560 --> 00:39:59.859
So I think there is this that there is an
interesting role for memory.

00:39:59.859 --> 00:40:05.660
I don't you know, I don't think of it personally
as being in competition with morality per

00:40:05.660 --> 00:40:06.660
se.

00:40:06.660 --> 00:40:08.740
But I don't study morality so that might be
why.

00:40:08.740 --> 00:40:12.880
Well I would actually suggest that this work
is not encompassing of what you're saying

00:40:12.880 --> 00:40:14.750
with what you're saying in a very different
sense.

00:40:14.750 --> 00:40:18.019
So what the three of us have been studying
is the question, how do people ordinarily

00:40:18.019 --> 00:40:19.049
understand the self?

00:40:19.049 --> 00:40:22.170
And then that's a very different question
from understanding how it is that human beings

00:40:22.170 --> 00:40:23.930
actually perform actions?

00:40:23.930 --> 00:40:28.049
So I feel like, suppose that we found that
people who ordinarily understand the self

00:40:28.049 --> 00:40:31.859
as being considered in such way that some
certain thing is the most fundamental thing

00:40:31.859 --> 00:40:35.990
and then you found that in fact, that thing
just is the thing that most fundamentally

00:40:35.990 --> 00:40:36.990
guides people's actions.

00:40:36.990 --> 00:40:40.910
In a way I think that would make our discovery
less interesting because then it wouldn't

00:40:40.910 --> 00:40:44.180
be that surprising that people would think
that's most fundamental, because it just simply

00:40:44.180 --> 00:40:45.800
is what's most fundamental.

00:40:45.800 --> 00:40:50.010
What would make-what would be most surprising
on the part of the- for us to discover is

00:40:50.010 --> 00:40:53.529
if people seem to all be converging on something
that's the absolutely most fundamental part

00:40:53.529 --> 00:40:54.529
of the self.

00:40:54.529 --> 00:40:58.029
But then when neuroscientists actually begin
to study it, they found that plays little

00:40:58.029 --> 00:40:59.029
role, if anything.

00:40:59.029 --> 00:41:02.460
So it seems like far from being there being
a competition if we find different things,

00:41:02.460 --> 00:41:05.569
it seems like it would make the work all the
more exciting.

00:41:05.569 --> 00:41:10.049
If these different kinds of research programs
ended up finding different things as beings

00:41:10.049 --> 00:41:11.049
what's at the core.

00:41:11.049 --> 00:41:16.579
I think that's largely what's happened in
my opinion with consciousness to some extent.

00:41:16.579 --> 00:41:17.579
It's interesting.

00:41:17.579 --> 00:41:18.610
We all think it's interesting.

00:41:18.610 --> 00:41:21.240
We all read about it and wonder about it.

00:41:21.240 --> 00:41:25.250
One of the findings emerging from neuroscience
and cognitive neuroscience and cognitive psychology

00:41:25.250 --> 00:41:30.079
is that it appears to play much less important
a role in many of our actions and behaviors

00:41:30.079 --> 00:41:32.829
and even reasoning than we thought it did.

00:41:32.829 --> 00:41:37.019
Right so suppose that people who study how
people ordinarily think about consciousness

00:41:37.019 --> 00:41:39.980
found that the way people ordinarily think
about consciousness is exactly the way that

00:41:39.980 --> 00:41:41.760
you think that the consciousness actually
works.

00:41:41.760 --> 00:41:45.630
Then the study of how people ordinarily think
about consciousness would be a slightly tedious

00:41:45.630 --> 00:41:46.650
research program.

00:41:46.650 --> 00:41:50.910
By contrast, suppose that you discover how
consciousness actually plays a role in people's

00:41:50.910 --> 00:41:54.760
actions and then people studying how people
think about consciousness find that it plays

00:41:54.760 --> 00:41:59.130
a rad-it plays a radically different role
in people's ordinary conception of their actions.

00:41:59.130 --> 00:42:00.130
Then things become interesting.

00:42:00.130 --> 00:42:04.320
We have to ask, how do people come to have
this conception of their actions that so radically

00:42:04.320 --> 00:42:08.329
departs from what our sense is telling us?

00:42:08.329 --> 00:42:10.470
Well it's just-sorry, Jesse.

00:42:10.470 --> 00:42:14.460
Nina if you want to talk a little bit about
the memory moral contract that's come out

00:42:14.460 --> 00:42:18.770
in some of your work.

00:42:18.770 --> 00:42:21.890
Oh well I guess I mentioned it in passing
before.

00:42:21.890 --> 00:42:28.309
But when we-when you ask-so you could give
people a scenario where you say imagine that

00:42:28.309 --> 00:42:34.750
it's the not too distant future and this man
Jim, he gets into a car accident and the doctors

00:42:34.750 --> 00:42:38.880
give him a little microchip to replace the
part of his brain that gets damaged.

00:42:38.880 --> 00:42:43.940
And then they do all these detailed tests
and psychological measures to see if he's

00:42:43.940 --> 00:42:48.819
OK and they find one of the following things:
they either find that he's psychologically

00:42:48.819 --> 00:42:54.890
normal, no change, or they find that he lost
all of his memories from before the accident,

00:42:54.890 --> 00:42:58.730
or they find that he's lost his moral compass,
he no longer knows the difference between

00:42:58.730 --> 00:42:59.730
right and wrong.

00:42:59.730 --> 00:43:04.670
And we've a few other conditions and we find
that if he's lost all his memories from before

00:43:04.670 --> 00:43:09.240
the accident, people say that's much less
important to the question of whether Jim is

00:43:09.240 --> 00:43:13.569
the same person now as before, as if he's
lost his moral compass.

00:43:13.569 --> 00:43:18.789
So certainly for folk judgments about the
nature of personal identity, morality plays

00:43:18.789 --> 00:43:24.960
a much larger role than memories, even if
it ends up not being the case for what actually

00:43:24.960 --> 00:43:26.530
drives behavior.

00:43:26.530 --> 00:43:31.580
When we when we first discovered this, it
really was striking from the perspective of

00:43:31.580 --> 00:43:33.140
someone trained in philosophy.

00:43:33.140 --> 00:43:38.260
If you study the theme of personal identity
and philosophy, the starting point is John

00:43:38.260 --> 00:43:40.260
Locke.

00:43:40.260 --> 00:43:44.200
In 1690, he wrote a book that really was the
book about the mind for the next 200 years.

00:43:44.200 --> 00:43:48.829
And in that, he was a very seminal discussion
of what makes you the same person over time.

00:43:48.829 --> 00:43:51.000
And it's a bit difficult to interpret this
whole text.

00:43:51.000 --> 00:43:52.730
What's the view.

00:43:52.730 --> 00:43:55.390
But it does look from his examples, like memory
is the key.

00:43:55.390 --> 00:43:59.579
What makes me the same person over time is
that I can recall my past.

00:43:59.579 --> 00:44:02.900
I can bring up these past episodes in my mind.

00:44:02.900 --> 00:44:07.519
So when we started to ask ordinary people
what they think about this, and Nina has done

00:44:07.519 --> 00:44:12.290
really at this point a couple of dozen, dozens
of experiments we have asking this question.

00:44:12.290 --> 00:44:14.740
They tend to think memory isn't that important.

00:44:14.740 --> 00:44:17.460
It could be a fanciful case or a very real
world case.

00:44:17.460 --> 00:44:24.359
We all have relatives who have suffered from
memory loss through aging and we have to ask

00:44:24.359 --> 00:44:25.359
is it the same person?

00:44:25.359 --> 00:44:31.080
And in all of this work, we find memory loss
does impact our sense of this being the loved

00:44:31.080 --> 00:44:32.650
one we've always had.

00:44:32.650 --> 00:44:35.049
But not to the same degree as change in values.

00:44:35.049 --> 00:44:40.050
Of all the things we've measured, and we keep
trying new things, morality just continues

00:44:40.050 --> 00:44:44.390
to show up as the trait for which identity
is framed.

00:44:44.390 --> 00:44:47.079
It really looks like the one that's most crucial.

00:44:47.079 --> 00:44:51.769
If you imagine an older relative whose values
start to change dramatically.

00:44:51.769 --> 00:44:56.770
That looks like they're less of the same person
than an older relative who's lost memory.

00:44:56.770 --> 00:45:01.119
And to me that really is the kind of discovery
that even though it's common sense that we're

00:45:01.119 --> 00:45:06.240
proving, all of our training and what identity
is has sort of gotten us to look past this

00:45:06.240 --> 00:45:11.300
core feature that's now emerging empirically
as central to the self.

00:45:11.300 --> 00:45:14.049
It's a great example of the value of experimental
philosophy.

00:45:14.049 --> 00:45:15.049
Right?

00:45:15.049 --> 00:45:20.820
Because for centuries, philosophers were focused
in on this one trait and as Jesse said, they're

00:45:20.820 --> 00:45:23.280
just looking past what actually is going on.

00:45:23.280 --> 00:45:27.980
John Locke had this one intuition that might
not be shared by you know the majority of

00:45:27.980 --> 00:45:28.980
humanity.

00:45:28.980 --> 00:45:30.200
Surprise, surprise philosophy.

00:45:30.200 --> 00:45:35.869
But it might be the relevant one for how people
actually make decisions.

00:45:35.869 --> 00:45:40.640
And I'm not you know I'm not sure I think
I think you know as Josh was saying earlier,

00:45:40.640 --> 00:45:44.710
these are slightly different angles on the
same question that are likely to disagree

00:45:44.710 --> 00:45:48.269
in interesting ways.

00:45:48.269 --> 00:45:53.869
And I guess you know one of my questions would
be why do we think that morality is not related

00:45:53.869 --> 00:45:54.869
to memory.

00:45:54.869 --> 00:45:59.609
I think one of the things I'd say is that
memory really informs a lot of behaviors,

00:45:59.609 --> 00:46:02.320
even when we're not aware that it does.

00:46:02.320 --> 00:46:09.890
So the idea that even moral that moral judgments
are not guided by or informed by memory also

00:46:09.890 --> 00:46:12.059
seems at least a question I think.

00:46:12.059 --> 00:46:18.299
Can I-I just want to push on this point that
you're making a little bit.

00:46:18.299 --> 00:46:26.030
So if you take someone who has radical memory
loss like H.M. or other sort of famous amnesic

00:46:26.030 --> 00:46:32.059
cases, is it not the case that they generally
have the same moral character as before?

00:46:32.059 --> 00:46:33.630
It's a little bit debatable actually.

00:46:33.630 --> 00:46:41.930
The original reports on people- so H.M. so
H.M. is a very famous patient who suffered

00:46:41.930 --> 00:46:47.119
memory loss and had an absolutely transformative
effect on our understanding of memory and

00:46:47.119 --> 00:46:48.520
the brain.

00:46:48.520 --> 00:46:53.740
He had a very severe case of epilepsy that
could not be treated pharmacologically with

00:46:53.740 --> 00:46:59.241
medical treatments, drug treatments, and so
they performed surgery on him when he was

00:46:59.241 --> 00:47:06.319
in his late 20s and removed the focus of where
the epileptic seizures were taking place.

00:47:06.319 --> 00:47:12.619
And that location often in epilepsy, is in
an area called the hippocampus which is kind

00:47:12.619 --> 00:47:16.440
of on the side of the brain inside the center
of the medial temporal lobe.

00:47:16.440 --> 00:47:24.019
The good news was that that surgical treatments
helped cure the epilepsy.

00:47:24.019 --> 00:47:30.539
The unexpected bad news was that H.M. was
no longer able to form new memories.

00:47:30.539 --> 00:47:34.859
So he didn't really lose memories from the
lifetime.

00:47:34.859 --> 00:47:40.230
But the doctor would come in every day and
every day H.M. didn't recognize the doctor.

00:47:40.230 --> 00:47:47.640
And if you try to probe H.M. to remember what
he did yesterday and tell you about it.

00:47:47.640 --> 00:47:48.640
He couldn't.

00:47:48.640 --> 00:47:55.150
He couldn't create new memories after losing
this part of the brain.

00:47:55.150 --> 00:48:02.009
And this was scientifically astonishing because
most of the theories in neuroscience until

00:48:02.009 --> 00:48:08.160
that point about localization of memory and
the brain go back to the previous century,

00:48:08.160 --> 00:48:12.480
had indicated from animal research that memories
are not in one part of the brain, they're

00:48:12.480 --> 00:48:14.029
distributed all over.

00:48:14.029 --> 00:48:18.079
And what happened to H.M revealed that no,
actually it is one part of the brain that

00:48:18.079 --> 00:48:23.530
seems to be important, crucially important
for one kind of memory which is kind of creating

00:48:23.530 --> 00:48:25.640
a record of what we would typically think
of memory.

00:48:25.640 --> 00:48:27.490
What you had for breakfast yesterday.

00:48:27.490 --> 00:48:34.180
Sorry, kind of went into teaching mode.

00:48:34.180 --> 00:48:40.860
The very interesting thing has happened with
H.M. and it's actually exciting to have the

00:48:40.860 --> 00:48:45.789
opportunity talk about a H.M. today because
one of the most famous scientists who interrogated

00:48:45.789 --> 00:48:51.509
H.M.'s memory over a lifetime was Sue Corkin
from MIT and she passed away about a week

00:48:51.509 --> 00:48:56.680
ago and H.M. passed away several years ago
and so anybody who's interested, there's a

00:48:56.680 --> 00:48:57.730
lot to read about it.

00:48:57.730 --> 00:49:03.349
And Sue Corkin did amazing work and kept on
interrogating him, and doing research on his

00:49:03.349 --> 00:49:04.349
memory.

00:49:04.349 --> 00:49:08.140
And that's turned out to be really interesting,
because the first passed result led to the

00:49:08.140 --> 00:49:14.660
consensus that this part of the brain was
specialized for what people call declarative

00:49:14.660 --> 00:49:15.660
memory.

00:49:15.660 --> 00:49:19.450
Is the ability to remember what happened yesterday
or the day before and that everything else

00:49:19.450 --> 00:49:25.100
was intact, like imagination and creativity
and morality and so forth.

00:49:25.100 --> 00:49:28.800
Over the years, that turned out to not be
true.

00:49:28.800 --> 00:49:36.430
And that there were a lot of other more subtle
changes in H.M.'s behavior and since then,

00:49:36.430 --> 00:49:44.119
we have many more people that have damage
to the hippocampus because of hypoxia or encephalitis,

00:49:44.119 --> 00:49:46.690
various other situations.

00:49:46.690 --> 00:49:50.310
And these people suffer, many of them from
memory loss.

00:49:50.310 --> 00:49:56.460
But understanding that what that memory is
turns out to be much more complicated.

00:49:56.460 --> 00:50:03.210
And one example that I think is related potentially
to morality, although again you know I don't

00:50:03.210 --> 00:50:08.460
know, tell me what you think, is that if you
just ask people to imagine a scenario, a hypothetical

00:50:08.460 --> 00:50:13.569
scenario which it sounds like often happens
in sort of assessments of moral judgments,

00:50:13.569 --> 00:50:17.880
that without a hippocampus, people don't imagine
that scenario in the same way.

00:50:17.880 --> 00:50:19.970
They imagine it in a very impoverished way.

00:50:19.970 --> 00:50:24.309
So if I just asked you I imagine this event
is unfolding in two years.

00:50:24.309 --> 00:50:26.039
Tell me about it.

00:50:26.039 --> 00:50:28.510
It's your-it's your birthday in two years.

00:50:28.510 --> 00:50:31.980
Most of us go into a lot of detail and apparently
vividly imagine it.

00:50:31.980 --> 00:50:38.310
People with damage to the hippocampus, which
our textbooks tell us is just memory, don't

00:50:38.310 --> 00:50:39.890
imagine that in detail at all.

00:50:39.890 --> 00:50:44.200
They give a very, very vague kind of sense
of what's going to happen.

00:50:44.200 --> 00:50:49.960
And so I think that's relevant here just for
thinking about memory as playing into something

00:50:49.960 --> 00:50:53.930
that you know it had no business playing into
based on the way we understood things initially

00:50:53.930 --> 00:50:58.510
from H.M., which I imagine, here you can tell
me that I'm wrong.

00:50:58.510 --> 00:51:01.350
Must be important for things like moral judgments.

00:51:01.350 --> 00:51:06.099
A couple of brief comments one first, very
much in agreement.

00:51:06.099 --> 00:51:12.309
A former student of mine named Felipe De Brigard
who's now a professor of neuroscience and

00:51:12.309 --> 00:51:16.930
philosophy at Duke wrote a dissertation where
he asked the question, what is memory for?

00:51:16.930 --> 00:51:18.579
And we all might think that's obvious.

00:51:18.579 --> 00:51:19.910
Memory is for remembering.

00:51:19.910 --> 00:51:22.480
It's for recalling the past.

00:51:22.480 --> 00:51:27.009
But what function does it have in evolutionary
terms to revisit all these episodes.

00:51:27.009 --> 00:51:30.190
Is nostalgia that useful evolutionarily?

00:51:30.190 --> 00:51:34.579
And the answer that he arrived at really systematizing
a lot of trends in the memory literature that

00:51:34.579 --> 00:51:36.029
you're involved with.

00:51:36.029 --> 00:51:38.869
Is that memory is about the future, not the
past.

00:51:38.869 --> 00:51:39.960
Memory is really about planning.

00:51:39.960 --> 00:51:44.780
It's about using our past to make decisions
about how to act in the future.

00:51:44.780 --> 00:51:47.630
Deprive someone of memory and that will be
impaired.

00:51:47.630 --> 00:51:53.180
I had ended(?) whole thing as a colleague
for number of years who was one of the pioneers

00:51:53.180 --> 00:51:57.180
studying this kind of memory and studying-worked
with H.M. and other patients with amnesia.

00:51:57.180 --> 00:52:01.480
And I asked Tobin(?) you know does H.M. really
have a moral life?

00:52:01.480 --> 00:52:06.579
And his answer at the time, just based on
conversations was that there's a way in which

00:52:06.579 --> 00:52:09.250
his sense of his life is very superficial.

00:52:09.250 --> 00:52:11.759
You ask any of us what's-what is your life
about?

00:52:11.759 --> 00:52:12.980
What are your projects?

00:52:12.980 --> 00:52:14.500
What are you in this for?

00:52:14.500 --> 00:52:19.910
Camus begins one of his books saying the most
important philosophical question is suicide.

00:52:19.910 --> 00:52:20.910
Why do we go on?

00:52:20.910 --> 00:52:22.400
Life has no meaning so why bother?

00:52:22.400 --> 00:52:25.070
Why face tomorrow?

00:52:25.070 --> 00:52:30.220
And I think all of us can reflect on that
question deeply in ways that are informed

00:52:30.220 --> 00:52:31.640
by our past.

00:52:31.640 --> 00:52:36.289
And that's part of our morality, our decisions,
what kind of person I want to be can guide

00:52:36.289 --> 00:52:38.119
my sense of meaning in life.

00:52:38.119 --> 00:52:43.200
I think for somebody who doesn't have access
to the past, that's going to be limited and

00:52:43.200 --> 00:52:47.900
the kind of answer that I think that H.M.
would give to Camus's questions would look

00:52:47.900 --> 00:52:50.720
very different than the kinds of answers that
we would all give.

00:52:50.720 --> 00:52:56.010
So I do agree morality just as you pointed
out, that reason and emotion are not separated

00:52:56.010 --> 00:52:57.010
in the brain.

00:52:57.010 --> 00:53:01.410
I think memory and morality are not fundamentally
separated in the brain.

00:53:01.410 --> 00:53:05.690
I also agree, but I also have something that
I'd like to add.

00:53:05.690 --> 00:53:10.710
This is reminding me of which is this old
Oliver Sacks essay and I know there was a

00:53:10.710 --> 00:53:15.630
tribute to him at this festival earlier this
week.

00:53:15.630 --> 00:53:18.579
And he wrote this wonderful essay, if you
haven't read it, you should go out and just

00:53:18.579 --> 00:53:21.859
leave the festival right now and read it right
now.

00:53:21.859 --> 00:53:28.309
It's called The Lost Mariner where he discusses
a patient with Korsakoff's syndrome and people

00:53:28.309 --> 00:53:32.579
who are familiar with Sacks' work will know
that one question that he was really obsessed

00:53:32.579 --> 00:53:35.510
with is the question of personal identity.

00:53:35.510 --> 00:53:38.391
And this whole essay is about this man with
Korsakoff's syndrome.

00:53:38.391 --> 00:53:45.480
Korsakoff's syndrome is the result of being
an alcoholic after many years.

00:53:45.480 --> 00:53:46.779
I think it's a vitamin B deficiency.

00:53:46.779 --> 00:53:50.200
The temporal lobes just disintegrate.

00:53:50.200 --> 00:53:57.220
And this patient, Jimmy, he had completely
lost not only his ability to form new memories

00:53:57.220 --> 00:54:01.480
but also almost all of his memories from the
past as well.

00:54:01.480 --> 00:54:05.819
And so he lived in this sort of bizarre sort
of world where you couldn't live in the past,

00:54:05.819 --> 00:54:09.549
he couldn't plan for the future, and he was
really only barely hanging on by a thread

00:54:09.549 --> 00:54:11.720
in the present.

00:54:11.720 --> 00:54:17.299
And so the question that Sacks interrogates
is, so is Jimmy the same person?

00:54:17.299 --> 00:54:20.140
Or in what sense does he have any kind of
identity at all?

00:54:20.140 --> 00:54:22.530
And the beginning part-just you know spoiler
alert, I going to tell you what happens in

00:54:22.530 --> 00:54:24.300
this essay.

00:54:24.300 --> 00:54:27.339
He's really obsessed with this idea that you
know memories really are preventing Jimmy

00:54:27.339 --> 00:54:29.720
from truly existing in any real sense.

00:54:29.720 --> 00:54:36.329
But then he, in a twist, he observes Jimmy
taking the sacrament at church and he sees

00:54:36.329 --> 00:54:41.559
how he's transcended, sees how he reacts to
the holy music, and how he acts when he's

00:54:41.559 --> 00:54:47.309
praying and he says Jimmy is not a spiritual
casualty at all.

00:54:47.309 --> 00:54:52.859
Since he could still be moved morally and
religiously and spiritually, he still very

00:54:52.859 --> 00:54:54.900
much existed.

00:54:54.900 --> 00:54:58.420
And the fact that you know he doesn't have
his memory traces anymore, but that's just

00:54:58.420 --> 00:55:00.510
you know that's just machinery.

00:55:00.510 --> 00:55:01.819
That's not who he really is.

00:55:01.819 --> 00:55:06.000
And so he absolutely has survived despite
this devastating brain damage.

00:55:06.000 --> 00:55:08.970
So it would say that his true self is still
there.

00:55:08.970 --> 00:55:10.890
You might say, you might say...

00:55:10.890 --> 00:55:14.720
You might say that but not only might say
that, but that's the sort of key finding coming

00:55:14.720 --> 00:55:18.579
out of Nina's work about people who have neurodegenerative
diseases.

00:55:18.579 --> 00:55:20.859
Those who lose their memories, they're supposed
to still...

00:55:20.859 --> 00:55:21.859
Right.

00:55:21.859 --> 00:55:29.279
So if you go to people's families who suffered
brain damage and you ask them about what are

00:55:29.279 --> 00:55:31.779
the symptoms that this that the patient has
experienced?

00:55:31.779 --> 00:55:34.741
And also you know do they seem like a stranger
to you?

00:55:34.741 --> 00:55:36.319
Do you feel like you still know who they are?

00:55:36.319 --> 00:55:39.990
Do they seem like the same person deep down
or not?

00:55:39.990 --> 00:55:42.950
And then you build a big fancy model.

00:55:42.950 --> 00:55:46.940
What you find is the extent- and I think that
Jesse alluded to this earlier, the extent

00:55:46.940 --> 00:55:52.900
to which at least people are observing memory
loss actually doesn't predict at all whether

00:55:52.900 --> 00:55:55.250
you think this person has changed or not.

00:55:55.250 --> 00:56:00.720
The almost the only thing that matters is
the extent to which their moral capacities

00:56:00.720 --> 00:56:01.819
have changed.

00:56:01.819 --> 00:56:06.539
The one, the one symptom that we found also
made an independent contribution to identity.

00:56:06.539 --> 00:56:08.190
We weren't expecting it all.

00:56:08.190 --> 00:56:13.020
No philosophers or really almost any kind
of psychologists predicted this.

00:56:13.020 --> 00:56:17.799
And so we were also surprised that there is
a smaller but still significant effect of

00:56:17.799 --> 00:56:19.869
loss of language.

00:56:19.869 --> 00:56:26.150
So if you have a loved one who can no longer
speak or speak fluently with the patient,

00:56:26.150 --> 00:56:30.539
this is having a significant effect and whether
you would say it's the same person.

00:56:30.539 --> 00:56:34.470
We thought that was really interesting because
language just hasn't-really been absent from

00:56:34.470 --> 00:56:36.640
the discussion in literature.

00:56:36.640 --> 00:56:40.130
Well one of the things that you keep saying
though, is others think of them as the same

00:56:40.130 --> 00:56:41.130
person.

00:56:41.130 --> 00:56:42.130
What about themselves?

00:56:42.130 --> 00:56:46.509
You know if you are experiencing dementia,
do you still see yourself as the same person

00:56:46.509 --> 00:56:49.940
or is your fundamental sense of self altered?

00:56:49.940 --> 00:56:54.240
And I know that if you know there is work
and if you ask people this question before

00:56:54.240 --> 00:56:59.480
they experience any changes, a lot of people
will say you know I don't want that to happen.

00:56:59.480 --> 00:57:03.700
And a lot of people are still able to make
decisions, end of life decisions when they

00:57:03.700 --> 00:57:05.790
are in the early stages of dementia.

00:57:05.790 --> 00:57:11.779
There have been some really lovely pieces
of writing on people who said, "I'm committing

00:57:11.779 --> 00:57:16.990
suicide here because I'm losing part of myself
every single day.

00:57:16.990 --> 00:57:19.470
This is when I can still make the decision.

00:57:19.470 --> 00:57:20.920
So I'm going to end my life."

00:57:20.920 --> 00:57:21.920
And they do.

00:57:21.920 --> 00:57:25.990
So it seems that to them, even though to other
people, other people might not agree that

00:57:25.990 --> 00:57:27.759
they still think that you're in there somewhere.

00:57:27.759 --> 00:57:29.019
You don't agree.

00:57:29.019 --> 00:57:30.980
You think that you are being lost.

00:57:30.980 --> 00:57:35.140
I think it's a very interesting dichotomy
if you're if you're talking about the self

00:57:35.140 --> 00:57:39.299
as external versus the self as perceived by
you.

00:57:39.299 --> 00:57:44.430
And it's really tricky methodologically if
you are trying to you know if you're trying

00:57:44.430 --> 00:57:48.930
to compare say what's the effect of memory
loss versus morality or any other cognitive

00:57:48.930 --> 00:57:52.749
capacity on your own sense of personal continuity
for a few reasons.

00:57:52.749 --> 00:57:56.710
We would love to just run this study again
on the patients themselves.

00:57:56.710 --> 00:57:58.809
There's a few problems.

00:57:58.809 --> 00:58:03.130
One is, when you lose your memories, you begin
to forget who you used to be.

00:58:03.130 --> 00:58:08.410
Another problem which might be even an even
bigger problem is that most people with different

00:58:08.410 --> 00:58:10.559
forms of dementia don't know that they're
sick.

00:58:10.559 --> 00:58:11.740
Especially past the very early stages.

00:58:11.740 --> 00:58:16.009
So if you ask them, "Are you the same person
as before or even do you have a disease?"

00:58:16.009 --> 00:58:17.279
They say, "no I'm fine.

00:58:17.279 --> 00:58:18.339
Nothing's wrong."

00:58:18.339 --> 00:58:25.029
So it becomes very difficult to figure out
what's actually going on because people are

00:58:25.029 --> 00:58:29.089
no longer able to refer in an objective way
to the person that they once were.

00:58:29.089 --> 00:58:34.099
I mean this is something that I was thinking
about obviously with Muhammad Ali's death

00:58:34.099 --> 00:58:38.250
that he was the same person that Muhammad
Ali we all knew?

00:58:38.250 --> 00:58:40.009
And I know you study Parkinson's.

00:58:40.009 --> 00:58:46.070
So it would be interesting to me that is there
a tipping point you know where-where people

00:58:46.070 --> 00:58:49.569
lose themselves?

00:58:49.569 --> 00:58:52.230
It's an interesting question, especially since
I think what you know is coming out of this

00:58:52.230 --> 00:58:55.579
discussion is well are they losing their sense
of themselves?

00:58:55.579 --> 00:58:58.579
Are their family losing them, their sense
of self?

00:58:58.579 --> 00:59:01.210
Are their actions and decisions actually changing
or not?

00:59:01.210 --> 00:59:06.990
Right so these are all different measures
and they don't have to agree.

00:59:06.990 --> 00:59:13.450
Parkinson's disease is in many ways particularly
scientifically interesting disease in the

00:59:13.450 --> 00:59:17.940
sense that-that the main thing that happens
in Parkinson's disease is a loss of dopamine

00:59:17.940 --> 00:59:21.720
neurons in the mid brain that project to a
target called the striatum.

00:59:21.720 --> 00:59:24.160
And so that's something that's been known
for a while.

00:59:24.160 --> 00:59:29.720
What's been interesting is the discovery that
when people show up with the very first mildest,

00:59:29.720 --> 00:59:35.240
earliest symptoms of Parkinson's disease,
they've already lost 70 to 80 percent of those

00:59:35.240 --> 00:59:36.340
neurons.

00:59:36.340 --> 00:59:41.410
So that tells us something we don't really
know how to interpret well about redundancy

00:59:41.410 --> 00:59:43.420
at least in this in this particular circuit.

00:59:43.420 --> 00:59:48.710
And just the question of tipping point suggests
that at least in terms of the brain circuit,

00:59:48.710 --> 00:59:50.680
there is a tipping point for motor control.

00:59:50.680 --> 00:59:53.260
Those are the symptoms that people show up
with.

00:59:53.260 --> 01:00:01.230
A lot of the work we've done has to look-has
been looking at how about that loss of dopamine

01:00:01.230 --> 01:00:06.109
changes people's ability to sort of gradually
learn associations over time.

01:00:06.109 --> 01:00:10.849
This is interesting to us because it's a form
of learning that's very different from what

01:00:10.849 --> 01:00:12.049
we would typically call memory.

01:00:12.049 --> 01:00:16.740
This has nothing to do with what you did yesterday
and this is the kind of learning that patients

01:00:16.740 --> 01:00:23.090
like H.M. typically are pretty much OK with
and depend on memory.

01:00:23.090 --> 01:00:30.040
And you know that it's very hard for me to
relate that to self in any kind of meaningful

01:00:30.040 --> 01:00:34.490
way without getting completely lost in speculation.

01:00:34.490 --> 01:00:41.569
And you know what we have done, we have some
data on how people do, people with Parkinson's,

01:00:41.569 --> 01:00:47.579
do this sort of implicit learning and how
well their families seem to think they're

01:00:47.579 --> 01:00:50.880
functioning and how much they think they've
changed.

01:00:50.880 --> 01:00:56.970
And those two things do seem to go together
so that the family's perception of how motivated

01:00:56.970 --> 01:01:02.749
a patient with Parkinson's is to engage in
everyday life seems to be somewhat related

01:01:02.749 --> 01:01:08.890
at least to how well they do on this very
kind of boring lab kind of experiment or measurement.

01:01:08.890 --> 01:01:16.700
Just to comment on Ali in this particular
case, I think the tragedy of seeing his degeneration,

01:01:16.700 --> 01:01:18.309
has different loci.

01:01:18.309 --> 01:01:22.650
And one of them has to do with seeing this
incredible athlete who's lost that physical

01:01:22.650 --> 01:01:23.650
control.

01:01:23.650 --> 01:01:30.680
But I think much more devastating, much more
profound for those who were admirers of Ali,

01:01:30.680 --> 01:01:32.819
is that he had been this tremendous force
in civil rights.

01:01:32.819 --> 01:01:36.809
I mean he was an extraordinarily courageous
civil rights leader.

01:01:36.809 --> 01:01:44.400
And to see that outspoken, articulate, brilliant
leader enter a phase in life where he could

01:01:44.400 --> 01:01:45.450
no longer do that.

01:01:45.450 --> 01:01:50.849
We all know people who live in athletics or
in dance or other physically demanding careers

01:01:50.849 --> 01:01:52.510
won't be able to sustain that.

01:01:52.510 --> 01:01:56.200
But we might expect that kind of leadership
to be sustainable.

01:01:56.200 --> 01:02:02.779
And I think the you know the ravages that
he experienced through Parkinson's probably

01:02:02.779 --> 01:02:04.119
prevented that to some degree.

01:02:04.119 --> 01:02:07.269
There's some apathy associated with Parkinson's,
through some depression.

01:02:07.269 --> 01:02:10.630
Associated with Parkinson's some impact on
memory.

01:02:10.630 --> 01:02:14.940
So all of those things are really limiting
the social position he can play with respect

01:02:14.940 --> 01:02:16.260
to leadership.

01:02:16.260 --> 01:02:21.739
And for me anyway and I think for many others,
that was the most sad, the most the most significant

01:02:21.739 --> 01:02:24.380
shift in who he was as a public persona.

01:02:24.380 --> 01:02:27.720
Can I come back to this issue you were talking
about earlier.

01:02:27.720 --> 01:02:31.940
So you know back at the beginning of our discussion,
Jesse was suggesting this idea that the self

01:02:31.940 --> 01:02:34.730
is attached to some thing social, to our connection
with other people.

01:02:34.730 --> 01:02:38.819
But then there was sort of a fight brewing
because Nina was suggesting maybe it's not

01:02:38.819 --> 01:02:43.960
in the sense that people with psychopathy
could recognize the same sort of social impact

01:02:43.960 --> 01:02:47.279
but wouldn't have the same kind of intuition
about the self.

01:02:47.279 --> 01:02:49.470
So I feel like I really regret that we didn't
really get to continue the fight.

01:02:49.470 --> 01:02:53.880
I just want to see, what do you what do you
guys things further about this?

01:02:53.880 --> 01:02:58.009
Well part of the issue is that the jury's
still out and since I mean I don't have a

01:02:58.009 --> 01:02:59.079
dog in this fight.

01:02:59.079 --> 01:03:05.430
So however the data turn out is what will
be where I end up you know laying my chips.

01:03:05.430 --> 01:03:14.890
But that being said, I don't know when you
bet you can lay your chips after the bet.

01:03:14.890 --> 01:03:19.650
It does seem-so one way of sort of rectifying
this or like bringing these two things together

01:03:19.650 --> 01:03:24.690
is to think maybe there is a proximate level
or the level of the mechanism.

01:03:24.690 --> 01:03:30.289
And that's the part that's getting disrupt-that's
instantiated maybe just as you think within

01:03:30.289 --> 01:03:32.380
yourself, well what do I value?

01:03:32.380 --> 01:03:36.010
And then I'll say that that applies to other
people's identity of what's important for

01:03:36.010 --> 01:03:37.010
them.

01:03:37.010 --> 01:03:43.250
But maybe at a more an ultimate functional
or even evolutionary level, it-the whole reason

01:03:43.250 --> 01:03:49.599
why this mechanism was put in place or set
into motion is because we care about social

01:03:49.599 --> 01:03:50.779
relationships so much.

01:03:50.779 --> 01:03:56.040
And actually in some new studies that we have,
when you make you know one of these big fancy

01:03:56.040 --> 01:04:01.460
models again and we pit- so if you ask an
individual about you know how much would their

01:04:01.460 --> 01:04:02.460
identity change?

01:04:02.460 --> 01:04:04.410
But then we also ask a bunch of other questions.

01:04:04.410 --> 01:04:08.420
How much would your social relationship with
someone change if this trait changed?

01:04:08.420 --> 01:04:09.869
How much how much do you value this?

01:04:09.869 --> 01:04:12.380
How much do you think is related to morality
and so forth.

01:04:12.380 --> 01:04:16.119
What you find is actually all of these things
matter a little bit.

01:04:16.119 --> 01:04:22.170
And social relationships actually also matter
at least when you when you're asking about

01:04:22.170 --> 01:04:23.339
judgments.

01:04:23.339 --> 01:04:28.329
So it's not I don't think the two are necessarily
in competition.

01:04:28.329 --> 01:04:31.160
I mean I like coming back to Josh's opening
comment.

01:04:31.160 --> 01:04:35.740
I do think we need to recognize that identity
is a construction.

01:04:35.740 --> 01:04:36.880
Maria used that word.

01:04:36.880 --> 01:04:37.989
I'm a social construction.

01:04:37.989 --> 01:04:41.570
So in the science world, there's the science
fans and then there is social constructionists.

01:04:41.570 --> 01:04:44.250
And they're supposed to sort of not get along.

01:04:44.250 --> 01:04:48.119
I'm a science grounded social constructionist
and partly because I think science is in the

01:04:48.119 --> 01:04:49.119
business of construction.

01:04:49.119 --> 01:04:50.680
So take the periodic table of the elements.

01:04:50.680 --> 01:04:56.140
It's supposed to be the most fundamental description
of the stuff that makes up the universe or

01:04:56.140 --> 01:04:59.289
a bit more fundamental but so what are these
elements?

01:04:59.289 --> 01:05:02.440
Well you could organize them by atomic weight.

01:05:02.440 --> 01:05:04.380
You could organize them by atomic number.

01:05:04.380 --> 01:05:08.470
You could include elements that actually exist
in the real world or ones that don't, merely

01:05:08.470 --> 01:05:10.759
hypothetical elements, or lab created elements.

01:05:10.759 --> 01:05:11.910
You could include isotopes.

01:05:11.910 --> 01:05:13.280
You could include ions.

01:05:13.280 --> 01:05:15.049
All these decisions need to be made.

01:05:15.049 --> 01:05:20.190
So it's considered elemental is actually a
set of arbitrary decisions.

01:05:20.190 --> 01:05:21.190
Not completely arbitrary.

01:05:21.190 --> 01:05:22.390
These are real things.

01:05:22.390 --> 01:05:26.200
But the way you describe the fundamental building
blocks of reality has a lot of degrees of

01:05:26.200 --> 01:05:27.200
freedom.

01:05:27.200 --> 01:05:31.130
The way we describe human identity has a lot
of degrees of freedom.

01:05:31.130 --> 01:05:33.549
And if you talk about so being a mother.

01:05:33.549 --> 01:05:37.279
Being a mother could be a matter of biological
connectedness or it could be a matter of playing

01:05:37.279 --> 01:05:38.450
a social role.

01:05:38.450 --> 01:05:39.450
Could a man be a mother?

01:05:39.450 --> 01:05:41.200
Could an adoptive parent be a mother?

01:05:41.200 --> 01:05:43.640
Those are questions that we get to settle
by decision.

01:05:43.640 --> 01:05:47.160
We get to settle questions of gender by decision,
questions of race by decision.

01:05:47.160 --> 01:05:50.450
And I think questions of personal identity
also by decision.

01:05:50.450 --> 01:05:53.599
And because it's based on decision, context
matters.

01:05:53.599 --> 01:05:57.239
There's work on how genes are defined in science
depends on the subfield.

01:05:57.239 --> 01:06:00.990
If you're in behavioral genetics it may work
differently than in some branches of molecular

01:06:00.990 --> 01:06:01.990
genetics.

01:06:01.990 --> 01:06:06.880
So I think we need to recognize that for many
of our purposes, morality might be very important

01:06:06.880 --> 01:06:08.779
for bookkeeping in human identity.

01:06:08.779 --> 01:06:10.349
For others, memory might be important.

01:06:10.349 --> 01:06:12.920
For others, various cognitive capacities may
be important.

01:06:12.920 --> 01:06:15.279
All of these things are equally real.

01:06:15.279 --> 01:06:17.749
All of them matter.

01:06:17.749 --> 01:06:21.819
But we get to decide given a goal which one
matters most.

01:06:21.819 --> 01:06:26.829
Well I think that's a very good point on which
to end this part of the discussion.

01:06:26.829 --> 01:06:29.630
Let's first give you guys a huge hand.

01:06:29.630 --> 01:06:38.230
We will open it up to you guys to ask your
questions.

01:06:38.230 --> 01:06:41.670
And there will be two mikes that will be traveling
around.

01:06:41.670 --> 01:06:42.670
Yes.

01:06:42.670 --> 01:06:43.670
Up here.

01:06:43.670 --> 01:06:51.760
Hold on please wait for the mike before asking
the question.

01:06:51.760 --> 01:06:53.950
Thank you for a great presentation.

01:06:53.950 --> 01:06:56.859
Now how, do you Jesse, define morality?

01:06:56.859 --> 01:07:00.279
What are the basic constituents of that?

01:07:00.279 --> 01:07:01.809
I think we need another hour for that.

01:07:01.809 --> 01:07:04.619
That's a big and hard question.

01:07:04.619 --> 01:07:09.150
I've come to think that morality is very connected
to emotions, which is something I alluded

01:07:09.150 --> 01:07:12.240
to at the beginning but didn't explain.

01:07:12.240 --> 01:07:15.309
So we have various different kinds of beliefs
about the world.

01:07:15.309 --> 01:07:18.069
Some of them are just you know factual beliefs.

01:07:18.069 --> 01:07:19.170
Dinosaurs are extinct.

01:07:19.170 --> 01:07:20.580
Aardvarks are marked nocturnal.

01:07:20.580 --> 01:07:22.789
Others are evaluative beliefs.

01:07:22.789 --> 01:07:24.349
And these include aesthetic values.

01:07:24.349 --> 01:07:25.630
This painting is beautiful.

01:07:25.630 --> 01:07:27.440
This one is ugly.

01:07:27.440 --> 01:07:31.579
Preference is for food, for friendship.

01:07:31.579 --> 01:07:36.940
Moral values are among these values and I
think the difference between fact and value,

01:07:36.940 --> 01:07:42.779
psychologically, is that values, expressions
of a preference of desire of beauty, but also

01:07:42.779 --> 01:07:46.950
of right and wrong fundamentally come down
to our feelings about things.

01:07:46.950 --> 01:07:51.509
So this is an old idea associated with people
like David Hume in The History of Philosophy

01:07:51.509 --> 01:07:58.490
that says morality is really an expression
of deeply socialized, strongly felt feelings.

01:07:58.490 --> 01:08:03.869
So when we train our children to be moral,
when we train our communities to be moral,

01:08:03.869 --> 01:08:06.829
we're trying to encourage them to be outraged
at injustice.

01:08:06.829 --> 01:08:13.460
To feel disgusted in criminality, to feel
pride and delight in generosity.

01:08:13.460 --> 01:08:15.099
It's all about the emotions.

01:08:15.099 --> 01:08:17.940
It's a very controversial view.

01:08:17.940 --> 01:08:24.020
Yeah, back there.

01:08:24.020 --> 01:08:26.040
Hi.

01:08:26.040 --> 01:08:34.960
So I think that the question that I have for
the gentleman with the awesome hair.

01:08:34.960 --> 01:08:36.200
All of you.

01:08:36.200 --> 01:08:43.730
When you were talking about projecting out
if your if your values, your political values

01:08:43.730 --> 01:08:47.290
for example change in the future that that
would be a big change of self.

01:08:47.290 --> 01:08:54.330
And I think there's a lot of research that
shows that we are we believe we will act more

01:08:54.330 --> 01:08:57.730
ideally further from now.

01:08:57.730 --> 01:09:02.620
We can take into account all of our every
day small decisions.

01:09:02.620 --> 01:09:08.940
So the question is, have you done research
where you look at people who've had big maybe

01:09:08.940 --> 01:09:14.510
political or ideological shifts and then ask
them, "have you changed?

01:09:14.510 --> 01:09:16.260
Has your inner self changed?"

01:09:16.260 --> 01:09:22.750
And do they just simply rationalize it with
language that yeah my large moral ideals have

01:09:22.750 --> 01:09:25.780
changed, but I'm still the same person?

01:09:25.780 --> 01:09:26.780
That's such a great question.

01:09:26.780 --> 01:09:30.510
The short answer is no and we'd love to-and
it's sort of on the on the agenda.

01:09:30.510 --> 01:09:35.320
It's difficult research to do partially because
of finding a big enough sample of people who

01:09:35.320 --> 01:09:39.230
have undergone those changes in an independent
way to measure that they have in fact undergone

01:09:39.230 --> 01:09:40.230
those changes.

01:09:40.230 --> 01:09:44.280
I-one of the things that got me interested
in this topic, I started thinking about the

01:09:44.280 --> 01:09:46.040
concept of moral death.

01:09:46.040 --> 01:09:50.410
So we think about this and dramatic changes
so that most of the neo- the architects of

01:09:50.410 --> 01:09:53.840
neo- conservatism began as really left wing
liberal.

01:09:53.840 --> 01:09:56.501
And then they-so what happens what happens
there?

01:09:56.501 --> 01:09:58.290
Are they the same people?

01:09:58.290 --> 01:10:02.370
Was Patty Hearst the same person when she
was so-called brainwashed?

01:10:02.370 --> 01:10:04.240
When people undergo religious transformation.

01:10:04.240 --> 01:10:07.360
So consider the newborn Christian phenomenon.

01:10:07.360 --> 01:10:12.610
Is that really a kind of death of an old self
and a beginning of a new self?

01:10:12.610 --> 01:10:17.630
We know that from a third person perspective,
people judge these things to be changes in

01:10:17.630 --> 01:10:18.910
identity.

01:10:18.910 --> 01:10:24.750
We know from first person perspective hypothetically,
when people are asked if you change in this

01:10:24.750 --> 01:10:26.230
way, would you be the same person?

01:10:26.230 --> 01:10:27.760
We tend to say no.

01:10:27.760 --> 01:10:32.050
But to actually interview people who have
undergone those transformations is a very

01:10:32.050 --> 01:10:35.890
I think logical and exciting direction for
us to move.

01:10:35.890 --> 01:10:38.400
So you're really anticipating where we where
we want to end up.

01:10:38.400 --> 01:10:40.680
So
hopefully the next panel will have an answer

01:10:40.680 --> 01:10:42.910
to that question.

01:10:42.910 --> 01:10:49.160
So back there?

01:10:49.160 --> 01:10:54.040
Around the era that cognitive science was
born, there was a lot of debate in the United

01:10:54.040 --> 01:11:00.630
States as Eastern traditions found a way in
as to whether the self was fundamental or

01:11:00.630 --> 01:11:06.690
not, whether we could deconstruct the self,
whether the self could even disappear, which

01:11:06.690 --> 01:11:13.000
was sort of an aspiration within at least
folk psychology of many Eastern traditions.

01:11:13.000 --> 01:11:17.660
And I wonder whether-most of what you talked
about seems to be about what aspects of the

01:11:17.660 --> 01:11:19.260
self seem to be malleable.

01:11:19.260 --> 01:11:25.410
But I wondered whether there is any of your
research that reflects upon this question

01:11:25.410 --> 01:11:32.210
about whether the self can be dismantled either
for a short period of time or in some extended

01:11:32.210 --> 01:11:33.210
way?

01:11:33.210 --> 01:11:34.210
Yeah.

01:11:34.210 --> 01:11:42.540
So I have some work with the philosopher Shaun
Nichols and Jay Garfield where we go to Tibet

01:11:42.540 --> 01:11:51.850
and look at Buddhists and now Buddhist-in
Buddhism, there is the doctrine of no self.

01:11:51.850 --> 01:11:57.550
Where what this tenet says is that the-you
might think that we have a self that persists

01:11:57.550 --> 01:11:59.210
over time, but this is just an illusion.

01:11:59.210 --> 01:12:03.960
There really isn't a self in any meaningful
sense.

01:12:03.960 --> 01:12:08.850
And this is a doctrine in part that's meant
to have these positive consequences.

01:12:08.850 --> 01:12:14.980
So as soon as you let go of this sense of
self, you won't be selfish anymore.

01:12:14.980 --> 01:12:15.980
Right?

01:12:15.980 --> 01:12:18.240
Because there really is no distinction between
you or other people.

01:12:18.240 --> 01:12:19.790
You would be more generous.

01:12:19.790 --> 01:12:24.720
And also you should be less afraid of death
because you'll understand that death isn't

01:12:24.720 --> 01:12:25.720
anything special.

01:12:25.720 --> 01:12:29.680
The death of the physical body because the
self is you know being destroyed in its own

01:12:29.680 --> 01:12:32.930
way continuously over a biological life as
well.

01:12:32.930 --> 01:12:38.230
And there's nothing actually all that special
about the death of the physical body.

01:12:38.230 --> 01:12:43.700
Now so we run into this research thinking
that we were going to find this and that the

01:12:43.700 --> 01:12:49.310
Buddhists were going to be so enlightened
about these issues and it turns out we found

01:12:49.310 --> 01:12:50.310
two things.

01:12:50.310 --> 01:12:54.410
First, we found actually Buddhists are more
afraid of death.

01:12:54.410 --> 01:12:59.700
Specifically, they're more afraid of the destruction
of the self.

01:12:59.700 --> 01:13:02.490
So if you give them like a bunch of different
why are you afraid of death?

01:13:02.490 --> 01:13:06.770
They are terrified of the idea of themselves
being destroyed at death.

01:13:06.770 --> 01:13:09.660
And so this paradoxical effect.

01:13:09.660 --> 01:13:11.260
And there's another paradoxical effect.

01:13:11.260 --> 01:13:14.350
We give them a generosity task essentially.

01:13:14.350 --> 01:13:19.670
Would you give some lifesaving medicine to
other people or how much longer would they

01:13:19.670 --> 01:13:24.410
need to live in order for you to agree to
give over your medicine to them if you are

01:13:24.410 --> 01:13:26.190
also going to die?

01:13:26.190 --> 01:13:32.730
And we find that- there are comparison classes
with American subjects and also Indian subjects

01:13:32.730 --> 01:13:38.530
who are Hindu and we find that the Americans
and Indians, I mean everyone's selfish right.

01:13:38.530 --> 01:13:40.540
No one says oh I'll just give away my pill.

01:13:40.540 --> 01:13:44.810
But the but the Tibetan Buddhists they and
they're like, "No way I'm not going to."

01:13:44.810 --> 01:13:48.880
Not-in fact two thirds of these- and these
are Lamas too.

01:13:48.880 --> 01:13:52.720
They're in you know school to be trained to
be priests.

01:13:52.720 --> 01:13:57.820
That two thirds of them say there's no amount
of time that this other person could live

01:13:57.820 --> 01:14:01.320
that I would give my medicine over to them.

01:14:01.320 --> 01:14:05.200
So one possible-we were you know we were sort
of blown away by this.

01:14:05.200 --> 01:14:10.380
One possible explanation though is this relentless
focusing on you know how the self is always

01:14:10.380 --> 01:14:13.490
being destroyed is actually quite terrifying.

01:14:13.490 --> 01:14:17.570
And that just sort of makes people even more
like reactive and attached to the self.

01:14:17.570 --> 01:14:23.120
So it's possible although the jury's still
out, it's possible that this might not even

01:14:23.120 --> 01:14:28.340
be a worthy goal to try to destroy this illusion
of the self, even though it might be you know

01:14:28.340 --> 01:14:29.860
very much an illusion.

01:14:29.860 --> 01:14:32.980
So Josh I was wondering could you ever destroy
the true self?

01:14:32.980 --> 01:14:35.910
Or is that something that's not destroyable?
it's an interesting question.

01:14:35.910 --> 01:14:39.890
So people seem to have this intuition that
the true self is in some bizarre sense immutable.

01:14:39.890 --> 01:14:45.420
So people seem to have the feeling that whatever
is my true self at this moment, it will be

01:14:45.420 --> 01:14:47.350
my true self until the time that I die.

01:14:47.350 --> 01:14:50.120
It was my true self from the moment that I
was born.

01:14:50.120 --> 01:14:55.960
Of course, I could have these changes on the
surface where something that I had that sort

01:14:55.960 --> 01:14:59.870
of appeared to me, what I was like shifts
to something else.

01:14:59.870 --> 01:15:04.560
But those surface changes seem like they're
just sort of illusions that appears from the

01:15:04.560 --> 01:15:06.011
top of the water.

01:15:06.011 --> 01:15:09.560
But there's some deeper thing that sort of
remaining constant the whole time.

01:15:09.560 --> 01:15:12.860
Of course it's very unlikely that scientific
study of the self will show that there is

01:15:12.860 --> 01:15:14.610
such a thing but people really do think that.

01:15:14.610 --> 01:15:26.830
Maybe along the lines of the Hindu conception
that these Buddhists are attacking.

01:15:26.830 --> 01:15:32.260
We spoke previously about the almost separateness
between memory and morality.

01:15:32.260 --> 01:15:37.960
In the experiment that Nina mentioned with
the Jim, the car accident sufferer.

01:15:37.960 --> 01:15:44.190
And I wonder if, I mean morality, the moral
the moral compass is essentially the difference

01:15:44.190 --> 01:15:48.680
between right and wrong, which requires a
memory of what is right and what is wrong.

01:15:48.680 --> 01:15:54.970
A kind of mnemonics schemata that you apply
over the course of time and I wonder if it's

01:15:54.970 --> 01:16:03.730
possible that there's a sort of memory based
fabric underneath morality.

01:16:03.730 --> 01:16:07.710
And if so, how can that be tested empirically?

01:16:07.710 --> 01:16:11.620
Daphna, do you want to?

01:16:11.620 --> 01:16:14.200
Well that's, I agree.

01:16:14.200 --> 01:16:20.380
In the sense that I think I think there are
sort of memory fabric beneath almost many

01:16:20.380 --> 01:16:23.810
of our behaviors of that sort.

01:16:23.810 --> 01:16:28.500
I think you know that general statement is
very hard to test and I think that you know

01:16:28.500 --> 01:16:32.210
the fact that Nina brought up patient H.M.
is really useful for that.

01:16:32.210 --> 01:16:38.690
I actually think that the data from people
with dementia is less informative for this

01:16:38.690 --> 01:16:42.710
question in the sense that we know that what
dementia does, like what happened to H.M.,

01:16:42.710 --> 01:16:44.490
is the inability to create new memories.

01:16:44.490 --> 01:16:48.080
But people with dementia remember most of
their memories from a lifetime.

01:16:48.080 --> 01:16:50.680
So one would think that those might be the
most informative.

01:16:50.680 --> 01:16:58.050
There are some studies for example kind of
bolstering this perspective, right, that leaders

01:16:58.050 --> 01:17:04.380
tend to make decisions about real world crises
and events that are heavily biased by one

01:17:04.380 --> 01:17:07.960
event that happened when they were at a critical
period of their life.

01:17:07.960 --> 01:17:12.190
So there's a lot of memory-a lot of research
showing that events that happened in our late

01:17:12.190 --> 01:17:16.880
teens, early 20s to ourselves and to the world
around us have a very large disproportionate

01:17:16.880 --> 01:17:23.110
effect on how we think of what is the best
decision later on.

01:17:23.110 --> 01:17:27.970
I don't know if that's been done in the moral
context or not but that would be one way to

01:17:27.970 --> 01:17:34.270
start getting at this with studies looking
at kind of how moral judgments later in life

01:17:34.270 --> 01:17:36.960
are impacted by events in different phases.

01:17:36.960 --> 01:17:38.890
I agree.

01:17:38.890 --> 01:17:44.440
I mean I think there are potentially ways
to get an empirically-like looking at a H.M.,

01:17:44.440 --> 01:17:49.700
people like H.M. who had not been tested on
standard scales things like psychopathy, which

01:17:49.700 --> 01:17:51.460
look at sensitivity to the moral domain.

01:17:51.460 --> 01:17:54.260
So that would be a very straightforward way
to do it.

01:17:54.260 --> 01:17:58.830
But I think there is so much evidence in life
that these things are connected.

01:17:58.830 --> 01:18:04.570
If people in this room are all old enough
to recall the out come of the verdict of the

01:18:04.570 --> 01:18:06.140
O.J. Simpson trial.

01:18:06.140 --> 01:18:12.980
For African-Americans, the idea that evidence
had been planted was so credible and so vivid

01:18:12.980 --> 01:18:19.000
that the verdict-an acquittal in that case
would seem like a just decision.

01:18:19.000 --> 01:18:24.560
For a white population who are oblivious to
sort of systemic harassment of various kinds

01:18:24.560 --> 01:18:30.020
from law enforcement, an acquittal would be
a case of injustice.

01:18:30.020 --> 01:18:31.790
And so how do you make those decisions?

01:18:31.790 --> 01:18:36.830
A life long of experiences with people in
law enforcement, with members of a community

01:18:36.830 --> 01:18:41.850
who had such encounters inform that decision
very, very important ways.

01:18:41.850 --> 01:18:47.600
So I think moral understanding of flip, in
this case, opposite moral intuitions about

01:18:47.600 --> 01:18:51.480
the same case are clearly showing the effect
of memory here.

01:18:51.480 --> 01:18:56.830
There's a hand way in the back on that side.

01:18:56.830 --> 01:19:00.520
Hi, so I wrote this down so I wouldn't forget.

01:19:00.520 --> 01:19:06.990
For something like narrative structures which
seem to be obviously like a human construction,

01:19:06.990 --> 01:19:12.370
I don't know maybe I'm wrong, but has that
in any way been imprinted neurologically over

01:19:12.370 --> 01:19:14.600
time?

01:19:14.600 --> 01:19:17.270
It's a great question.

01:19:17.270 --> 01:19:18.360
There's a lot to say about it.

01:19:18.360 --> 01:19:23.930
But just let me give you one simple example
that speaks to narrative in memory.

01:19:23.930 --> 01:19:30.290
There has been work by Jeff Sachs and Lila
Davachi, who's actually here at NYU, showing

01:19:30.290 --> 01:19:36.770
that the way our brain organizes memories
is influenced by narrative in the sense of

01:19:36.770 --> 01:19:39.120
taking advantage of boundary shifts.

01:19:39.120 --> 01:19:43.290
So you can tell a story and connect one part
of the story to the other part of the story

01:19:43.290 --> 01:19:47.970
with the word "and' or you can tell a story
where instead of the "and" there is an "and

01:19:47.970 --> 01:19:52.140
then" or something like that that implies
a pause.

01:19:52.140 --> 01:19:56.750
And that the brain then kind of is more likely
to group together the events that happened

01:19:56.750 --> 01:20:00.370
in one part of the narrative separate from
the other part of the narrative based on these

01:20:00.370 --> 01:20:06.490
kind of hints on the narrative structure.

01:20:06.490 --> 01:20:12.570
Just a quick addition to that, in philosophy
there is a personal identity mostly involving

01:20:12.570 --> 01:20:13.570
memory.

01:20:13.570 --> 01:20:16.890
But the next most popular theory of that,
after that is a narrative theory which says,

01:20:16.890 --> 01:20:21.200
what makes me the same person over time is
my capacity to weave a kind of life story

01:20:21.200 --> 01:20:22.200
together.

01:20:22.200 --> 01:20:27.460
So with Shaun Nichols, we did a study to probe
ordinary intuitions about this.

01:20:27.460 --> 01:20:31.980
So we ask people to imagine somebody who could
still draw memories from the past but couldn't

01:20:31.980 --> 01:20:34.200
organize them into a coherent narrative.

01:20:34.200 --> 01:20:39.650
Maybe by using their capacities that Daphna
just described.

01:20:39.650 --> 01:20:40.960
Would he be the same person?

01:20:40.960 --> 01:20:44.400
If you can't organize your life into a story,
are you the same person?

01:20:44.400 --> 01:20:48.630
And overwhelmingly, we found that people answered
yes, that losing your narrative capacity is

01:20:48.630 --> 01:20:49.920
not a threat to the self.

01:20:49.920 --> 01:20:54.670
It's not much of a threat at all and certainly
not nearly as much of a threat as losing your

01:20:54.670 --> 01:20:55.760
moral values.

01:20:55.760 --> 01:20:59.970
One by one, we've gone through theories of
personal identity from philosophy and shown

01:20:59.970 --> 01:21:06.470
that none of them explains ordinary judgment
as much as continuity in morals.

01:21:06.470 --> 01:21:16.361
Yes the lady in the middle with the T-shirt.

01:21:16.361 --> 01:21:22.030
Thanks so much for just engaging each other
and us in a really interesting discussion.

01:21:22.030 --> 01:21:29.820
I was particularly interested by something
that you said Jesse, that in kind of looking

01:21:29.820 --> 01:21:37.250
at sort of the neural correlates of morality
based decisions, they involve like really

01:21:37.250 --> 01:21:40.610
emotional areas of the brain.

01:21:40.610 --> 01:21:49.540
What like how-kind of in sort of like historical
evolutionary brain development, how old or

01:21:49.540 --> 01:21:51.600
new are those areas?

01:21:51.600 --> 01:21:53.960
How human specific are they?

01:21:53.960 --> 01:21:55.330
Good question.

01:21:55.330 --> 01:21:56.740
I think they're very well concerned.

01:21:56.740 --> 01:22:02.380
In fact I think the-much of the much of the
neocortex is well conserved across mammalian

01:22:02.380 --> 01:22:03.380
phyla.

01:22:03.380 --> 01:22:09.420
So most-I mean you can use rats as models
for studies of just about every human psychiatric

01:22:09.420 --> 01:22:11.560
disorder just because there's so much conservation.

01:22:11.560 --> 01:22:16.080
There are important differences, many of them
are not well understood even if you take great

01:22:16.080 --> 01:22:21.380
apes, you can see things like the laminar
structure the layers of cells in visual areas

01:22:21.380 --> 01:22:25.850
which are very important for for primate cognition
are different than human beings.

01:22:25.850 --> 01:22:27.480
So there are differences.

01:22:27.480 --> 01:22:32.830
But I think that differences on are greatly
outweighed by the similarities.

01:22:32.830 --> 01:22:38.391
So the brain areas that get involved, orbital
frontal areas like Broadmann areas 9 and 10

01:22:38.391 --> 01:22:41.760
which are sort of right here if you drive
your fingers through your head.

01:22:41.760 --> 01:22:46.710
Temporal pole, which if you drive your fingers
through your temples, the entirety of cingulate

01:22:46.710 --> 01:22:50.940
cortex, which is one of the oldest areas of
the cortex which in the middle of the brain.

01:22:50.940 --> 01:22:55.470
Is just this, if you know what the corpus
collosum is, the bit that holds the hemispheres

01:22:55.470 --> 01:22:57.350
together and allows for communication.

01:22:57.350 --> 01:23:01.660
The bit of cortex that surrounds that comes
up in a lot of these studies.

01:23:01.660 --> 01:23:05.570
One area that might be a difference maker,
the superior tempora sulcus, which is very

01:23:05.570 --> 01:23:10.950
involved in aspects of social cognition like
attributing mental states to others is another

01:23:10.950 --> 01:23:13.540
big player in world cognition.

01:23:13.540 --> 01:23:17.460
These structures do have homologs in primates
at least.

01:23:17.460 --> 01:23:23.530
But we do know that our capacity to use these
structures to engage in high levels of social

01:23:23.530 --> 01:23:28.690
cognition is appreciably greater.

01:23:28.690 --> 01:23:35.140
So there is some possibility that there are
some distinctive human capacities involved

01:23:35.140 --> 01:23:36.140
in morality.

01:23:36.140 --> 01:23:40.770
I would say that non-human animals probably
don't have more rules in the way that we do.

01:23:40.770 --> 01:23:44.420
Partially because they lack this aspect of
social cognition.

01:23:44.420 --> 01:23:48.000
The really rich sense of how is this going
to make someone else feel?

01:23:48.000 --> 01:23:49.950
Those kinds of questions that are so important.

01:23:49.950 --> 01:23:51.570
What were the intentions behind this action?

01:23:51.570 --> 01:23:55.800
Was it an accident that you harmed that person
or did you fore-think that?

01:23:55.800 --> 01:24:01.010
Those things are so core to a lot of human
morality in ways that make me think the differences

01:24:01.010 --> 01:24:02.100
might matter.

01:24:02.100 --> 01:24:05.910
But with respect to the brain, I don't think
we're seeing those differences come out as

01:24:05.910 --> 01:24:06.910
of yet.

01:24:06.910 --> 01:24:07.910
I don't know if Daphna wants to...

01:24:07.910 --> 01:24:15.100
I think I'd just add that it's not-one question
is whether the areas exist which is important.

01:24:15.100 --> 01:24:18.900
Some newer studies are suggesting not for
morality per se but in general, but one thing

01:24:18.900 --> 01:24:21.510
that changes is how those areas talks each
other.

01:24:21.510 --> 01:24:26.570
Where the connections are even in areas that
all exist across species.

01:24:26.570 --> 01:24:31.350
And so that that turns out to be an important
dimension for understanding cognition thinking

01:24:31.350 --> 01:24:39.750
and reasoning in general.

01:24:39.750 --> 01:24:44.980
So there's data showing and that when evaluating
others identities, we pay more attention to

01:24:44.980 --> 01:24:50.230
morality than memory or personality or taste
or intelligence.

01:24:50.230 --> 01:24:56.830
And I'm wondering if it's even possible or
if it's possible to test which one of those

01:24:56.830 --> 01:25:00.350
is actually most predictive of behavior in
some general sense?

01:25:00.350 --> 01:25:03.180
If that's even an acute, coherent question.

01:25:03.180 --> 01:25:06.230
And if it is, how might you approach it?

01:25:06.230 --> 01:25:09.060
Josh, do you want to...?

01:25:09.060 --> 01:25:13.610
I think that a lot of the discussion we've
been having with the relationship between

01:25:13.610 --> 01:25:18.200
this idea we have of how to understand how
we ordinarily understand the self and the

01:25:18.200 --> 01:25:19.200
science of the self.

01:25:19.200 --> 01:25:22.920
So how people's actual behavior works, how
people actually perform behaviors.

01:25:22.920 --> 01:25:27.040
But I think if you want to understand how
people think about the self, I don't think

01:25:27.040 --> 01:25:30.810
that the real reason we think about the self
in the way that we do is because we have some

01:25:30.810 --> 01:25:34.910
specialized systems for human beings in the
way that for example we may have a specialized

01:25:34.910 --> 01:25:38.370
Department, the Department of Psychology,
that is in charge of understanding how human

01:25:38.370 --> 01:25:39.370
beings behave.

01:25:39.370 --> 01:25:41.480
Rather we have a general way of thinking about
things.

01:25:41.480 --> 01:25:43.450
We just apply that thing to the self.

01:25:43.450 --> 01:25:48.330
So in particular, we have a joke, the first
thing you are the essences of things so we

01:25:48.330 --> 01:25:51.850
can think about what is the essence of you
know Nina's.

01:25:51.850 --> 01:25:56.700
But we also think what is the essence of this
band?

01:25:56.700 --> 01:25:58.660
What is the essence of the university?

01:25:58.660 --> 01:26:00.450
What's the essence of science?

01:26:00.450 --> 01:26:01.630
What is the essence of the United States?

01:26:01.630 --> 01:26:04.910
And in each of those cases, we show this exact
same effect.

01:26:04.910 --> 01:26:09.470
So if you ask people, not what is the essence
of Jim, but what's the essence of the United

01:26:09.470 --> 01:26:10.470
States?

01:26:10.470 --> 01:26:11.600
What's the United States really all about?

01:26:11.600 --> 01:26:14.450
Then people have really different views about
what's good.

01:26:14.450 --> 01:26:18.120
If say that what the United States is really
about is that thing, the thing, the thing

01:26:18.120 --> 01:26:19.620
they think is the good thing.

01:26:19.620 --> 01:26:22.120
And then of course the United States does
all this other stuff.

01:26:22.120 --> 01:26:25.900
For different people, it's the opposite things
which they think is the other stuff.

01:26:25.900 --> 01:26:29.050
But whichever is the thing they think is the
bad thing of the United States they think

01:26:29.050 --> 01:26:30.440
that's just kind of some superficial phenomenon.

01:26:30.440 --> 01:26:33.150
If we could get back to the essence of what
the United States is really all about, it

01:26:33.150 --> 01:26:34.150
would be this good thing.

01:26:34.150 --> 01:26:39.930
To thinking, maybe in response to Matt's question
is that you shouldn't think of this capacity

01:26:39.930 --> 01:26:43.260
we have to think about the self as somehow
as if it were an attempt either successful

01:26:43.260 --> 01:26:45.840
or failed to do the psychology of itself.

01:26:45.840 --> 01:26:49.710
You should think of it as an application of
this much more general capacity we have to

01:26:49.710 --> 01:26:53.310
think about essences of about what things
are really about to the particular case of

01:26:53.310 --> 01:26:54.950
human beings.

01:26:54.950 --> 01:26:57.580
There's one very brief addition to Matt's
question.

01:26:57.580 --> 01:27:03.540
I mean I think and this came out in Daphna's
questions about the importance of behavior.

01:27:03.540 --> 01:27:07.350
We can ask ourselves in each case, will a
change in moral values affect behavior more

01:27:07.350 --> 01:27:08.380
than change in memory?

01:27:08.380 --> 01:27:09.980
And there's not going to be a fixed answer.

01:27:09.980 --> 01:27:13.330
There'll be certain domains where that difference
really does matter.

01:27:13.330 --> 01:27:17.780
So we've been looking at a little bit in the
context of parole cases.

01:27:17.780 --> 01:27:23.690
So to go back to a related case of past criminality,
our old friend Adolf Eichmann.

01:27:23.690 --> 01:27:28.370
So suppose Eichmann had been captured in Buenos
Aires some decades later.

01:27:28.370 --> 01:27:34.860
So he wasn't captured in the 60s, he was captured
say in the 80s and he was already experiencing

01:27:34.860 --> 01:27:35.860
dementia.

01:27:35.860 --> 01:27:42.690
So now you ask Eichmann, do you remember your
transgressions when you were working for the

01:27:42.690 --> 01:27:43.690
Reich?

01:27:43.690 --> 01:27:44.890
And he might have no memory of that.

01:27:44.890 --> 01:27:46.150
Imagine he has no memory of that.

01:27:46.150 --> 01:27:53.580
But imagine that he still is deeply committed
to his anti-Semitic agenda.

01:27:53.580 --> 01:27:59.110
And now ask yourself if he were given the
opportunity to re-offend, when he re-offend?

01:27:59.110 --> 01:28:01.150
And there it seems like the answer might be
yes.

01:28:01.150 --> 01:28:05.970
And merely forgetting your past crimes doesn't
necessarily reduce probability of re-offense

01:28:05.970 --> 01:28:08.170
in the same way that world reform would.

01:28:08.170 --> 01:28:11.530
So in the context of parole right now just
doing it with hypothetical cases but we're

01:28:11.530 --> 01:28:13.880
starting to do archive work too.

01:28:13.880 --> 01:28:16.090
The hope is that we can see the impact.

01:28:16.090 --> 01:28:21.100
We know that with parole decisions, change
in values really matters.

01:28:21.100 --> 01:28:25.620
And I think the reason for that is with respect
to this particular issue of criminal behavior,

01:28:25.620 --> 01:28:28.590
we know that that's the difference that makes
a difference.

01:28:28.590 --> 01:28:32.990
Whereas a loss of memory or maybe even in
some cognitive capacities wouldn't necessarily

01:28:32.990 --> 01:28:34.620
give the same reassurance.

01:28:34.620 --> 01:28:38.010
But if I may respectfully disagree.

01:28:38.010 --> 01:28:42.651
So what Jesse is saying, which I think is
a very reasonable hypothesis is that the reason

01:28:42.651 --> 01:28:46.610
people do this, the reason that for example
in parole decisions people care about this

01:28:46.610 --> 01:28:49.910
question, is because they care about behavior.

01:28:49.910 --> 01:28:51.940
They care about whether someone is going to
re-offend.

01:28:51.940 --> 01:28:54.050
And that would really make a lot of sense.

01:28:54.050 --> 01:28:56.010
But I don't think that's what's going on in
this case at all.

01:28:56.010 --> 01:28:57.520
It has nothing to do with what makes sense.

01:28:57.520 --> 01:29:01.410
It has to do with just our general ability
to think about things and whether something

01:29:01.410 --> 01:29:02.990
is the same thing as something else.

01:29:02.990 --> 01:29:07.440
So say Jesse commits some crime then we find
someone and we're trying to figure out whether

01:29:07.440 --> 01:29:08.440
that person has be convicted.

01:29:08.440 --> 01:29:10.750
We want to ask the question, is that person
Jesse?

01:29:10.750 --> 01:29:14.750
If it's not Jesse, if it's someone else, then
we can't convict him of the crime that Jesse

01:29:14.750 --> 01:29:17.010
can do-that was the one who performed it.

01:29:17.010 --> 01:29:22.150
So this study that Jesse mentioned a number
of minutes ago about someone who's either

01:29:22.150 --> 01:29:25.770
a punk and turns into a jock or a jock who
turns into a punk, is actually a story about

01:29:25.770 --> 01:29:27.280
this very phenomenon.

01:29:27.280 --> 01:29:31.080
So people think of punks and jocks as equally
willing to- equally likely to commit certain

01:29:31.080 --> 01:29:32.700
kinds of crimes.

01:29:32.700 --> 01:29:36.230
It's not as though punks commit crimes but
jocks don't, or jocks commit crimes but punks

01:29:36.230 --> 01:29:37.230
don't.

01:29:37.230 --> 01:29:41.750
But if you switch between them, so say you're
a punk and you commit a crime, you're shoplifting

01:29:41.750 --> 01:29:43.600
and then later you turn into a jock.

01:29:43.600 --> 01:29:45.860
People think less that you should be punished.

01:29:45.860 --> 01:29:49.340
Not because they think that now you turn into
a jock, you're less likely to shoplift again.

01:29:49.340 --> 01:29:53.270
But because they think they can't convict
that person of the crime who is not the same

01:29:53.270 --> 01:29:54.270
person anymore.

01:29:54.270 --> 01:29:58.570
It's not the punk who originally committed
the crime.

01:29:58.570 --> 01:30:03.880
Well I-we are unfortunately out of time even
though I could listen to you guys speak forever

01:30:03.880 --> 01:30:10.110
so let's get one more huge round of applause.

